// Generated by scripts/build-user-guide-pdf.py from docs/user-guide/USER_GUIDE.md. Do not edit.
export const GUIDE_HTML = "<section id=\"welcome-to-invento\" class=\"level2\">\n<h2>Welcome to Invento</h2>\n<p>Invento is Atharva’s system of record for everything between “a\nvendor delivers raw material” and “a finished, labelled, certified\nproduct is ready to ship.” It replaces paper registers and spreadsheets\nwith one connected system. Every raw material batch, quality check,\nrecipe, production run and certificate is entered once and then reused\nwherever it’s needed.</p>\n<p>This guide is for everyone who uses Invento day to day: recording a\ndelivery, running a quality check, building a batch or looking something\nup. You don’t need to read it cover to cover. Use the table of contents\nto jump to the screen you’re working on. To see how everything connects\nfirst, read <a\nhref=\"#the-big-picture-how-data-flows-through-invento\">The big\npicture</a>.</p>\n<p>Each module section below follows the same pattern, so you always\nknow where to look for a given kind of answer:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Where to find it</strong>: the exact menu path in the\nsidebar.</li>\n<li><strong>Who can use it</strong>: who can view the screen, and who\ncan add, edit, or delete on it.</li>\n<li><strong>What it’s for</strong>: the plain-language purpose of the\nscreen.</li>\n<li><strong>How to…</strong>: step-by-step instructions for the main\nthings you’ll do there.</li>\n<li><strong>Fields you’ll be asked for</strong>: what each important\nfield means and any rules around it.</li>\n<li><strong>How it connects to the rest of the app</strong>: what feeds\ninto this screen and what it feeds into.</li>\n<li><strong>Good to know</strong>: practical tips, edge cases, and\nthings that trip people up.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"getting-started\" class=\"level2\">\n<h2>Getting started</h2>\n<section id=\"signing-in\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Signing in</h3>\n<p>Go to Invento’s sign-in page and enter your email and password. A\nSystem Admin creates every account; you can’t register yourself. If you\ndon’t have an account yet, ask your System Admin. You’ll get a temporary\npassword and choose your own the first time you sign in. The roles the\nSystem Admin gives you decide which screens and actions you can use (see\n<a href=\"#what-your-role-lets-you-do\">What your role lets you do</a>).\nUntil you have a role, you can view the app but won’t see Add or Edit\nbuttons.</p>\n<p><strong>Automatic sign-out.</strong> To protect a PC left unattended,\nInvento signs you out after a period without activity (60 minutes unless\nyour System Admin has changed it, or switched it off). A reminder\nappears two minutes before, with a <strong>Stay signed in</strong>\nbutton. System Admins are not signed out this way. Anything you had not\nsaved is lost, so save long entries as you go.</p>\n<p>Forgot your password? Use the <strong>Forgot password</strong> link\non the sign-in page to receive a reset link by email, then set a new\npassword on the page it takes you to.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"finding-your-way-around\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Finding your way around</h3>\n<p>Once you’re signed in, you’ll always see the same layout:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sidebar (left)</strong>: every module you have access to, in\nseven groups: Overview, Master data, Procurement, Quality Control &amp;\nDocuments, Manufacturing, Reports &amp; checks (Reports, Audit Log,\nStock Integrity Check), and Admin. (SOP / STP Documents sits under Admin\nin the sidebar.)</li>\n<li><strong>Top bar (right)</strong>: your name and assigned role(s), a\nlive <strong>low stock</strong> alert badge when any item has fallen\nbelow its reorder threshold (click it to jump straight to Item Master),\na <strong>Profile</strong> link, and <strong>Sign out</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Main area</strong>: the screen you’re currently on.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Lists and tables.</strong> Every list has a search box above\nit. Where a column heading shows a small up/down mark, click it to sort\nby that column. Click again to reverse the order, and a third time to go\nback to the original order. The sort covers the whole list, not just the\npage on screen, and the Excel and PDF downloads too. On a phone, the\nmenu and buttons are larger, the first column stays in view while you\nswipe a wide list sideways, and a note shows when there is more to the\nright. Keyboard users can press Tab once on any page to reveal a\n<strong>Skip to main content</strong> link.</p>\n<p><strong>PDF downloads.</strong> PDFs print the rupee sign and letters\nwith marks correctly; Hindi text prints too, without the joined letter\nforms. A PDF holds at most 3,000 rows: if a list is longer, narrow it\nfirst or use the Excel download.</p>\n<p><strong>Show inactive.</strong> The Vendor Master, Instrument /\nEquipment Master and Dead Stock Register lists hide inactive records\nuntil you tick <strong>Show inactive</strong> above the list (the tick\nappears only when at least one record is inactive). Inactive records\ncarry a grey Inactive tag, and the Excel download has a Status\ncolumn.</p>\n<p><strong>Two people editing the same record.</strong> On the Item,\nVendor and purchase order invoice-details forms, if someone else saves\nthe same record while you are editing it, your save is refused with\n“Someone else changed this record while you were editing it. Nothing was\nsaved. Reload the page to see their change, then make your edit again.”\nReload, check their change and enter yours again.</p>\n<p><strong>Typing a date.</strong> Every date box reads\n<strong>dd-mm-yyyy</strong> (day first), whatever language your computer\nuses. Type it as 05-10-2026, 5/10/2026 or 05102026, or click the\ncalendar at the right of the box. If the date does not exist or is\noutside the allowed range, the box says so when you save.</p>\n<p><strong>Dates and limits.</strong> A date for something that has\nalready happened can’t be in the future. That covers the invoice date,\nreceived on, batch start and finish, issue date, calibration, dead stock\npurchase and resolution, and the report and audit filters. Quantities,\nprices and text have upper limits too, for example 1,000,000 kg or ltr,\na unit price of ₹10,000,000 and names of 200 characters. The message\nnames the field to correct.</p>\n<p>At the bottom of every page is a <strong>Feedback &amp; change\nlog</strong> box. It’s the fastest way to report something confusing or\nbroken from the place you noticed it. See <a\nhref=\"#tester-feedback\">Tester Feedback</a> for details.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"your-profile\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Your profile</h3>\n<p>Click <strong>Profile</strong> in the top bar to see your account\ndetails (email and roles) and your display name, or to change your\npassword; it asks for your current password first. Only the System\nAdministrator can change your display name (ask them on User Roles &amp;\nAccess), because it appears on QC decisions, certificates and the audit\ntrail.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"first-time-orientation\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>First-time orientation</h3>\n<p>If you’re brand new to Invento, the fastest way to get oriented\nis:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Open the <strong>Dashboard</strong> (the first screen after you sign\nin) to see what’s in the system now: stock, pending quality checks and\nrecent production.</li>\n<li>Read <a href=\"#the-big-picture-how-data-flows-through-invento\">The\nbig picture</a> below to understand how a batch of raw material moves\nthrough the app from delivery to finished product.</li>\n<li>Check <a href=\"#what-your-role-lets-you-do\">What your role lets you\ndo</a> to see which specific screens and actions apply to your job.</li>\n<li>Jump to the module section for whatever you’re about to do.</li>\n</ol>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section id=\"the-big-picture-how-data-flows-through-invento\"\nclass=\"level2\">\n<h2>The big picture: how data flows through Invento</h2>\n<p>Invento follows one continuous chain. Knowing it is the most useful\nthing you can learn about the app: almost every “why can’t I do X”\nquestion comes down to where a record is in this chain.</p>\n<p><strong>1. Purchase: material arrives.</strong> A purchase order\nrecords a vendor delivery, with one line per item received. While it’s a\nDraft, it doesn’t affect stock, and you can edit or delete its lines.\n<strong>Submit order (locks lines)</strong> is the step that matters: it\nlocks the order and adds the received quantity, less the QC, Stability\nand R&amp;D samples, to inventory as a new batch. A System Admin can\nreopen a submitted order, but only until QC has started on one of its\nbatches.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Quality Control: material is cleared for use.</strong>\nEvery batch from Purchase must pass Quality Control before anyone can\nuse it. QC is a <strong>two-round check</strong>: a Quality Checker\nreviews the batch first and, only if they approve, a <em>different</em>\nperson, the QC Reviewer, makes the final decision. A batch that is\nrejected, still pending, expired or overdue for retest is\n<strong>blocked everywhere else in the app</strong>. The app itself\nwon’t let you pick it when you build something downstream.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Inventory Ledger: stock is tracked.</strong> Every stock\nmovement goes into one ledger automatically: material received, QC\nsamples, material used in production, packaging issued and wastage.\nCheck here what’s on hand now, or what was on hand on a past date.</p>\n<p><strong>4. MFR: the recipe is defined and approved.</strong> A\nManufacturing Formula Record (MFR) is the approved recipe for a finished\nproduct: which raw materials, and how much of each, make one batch. An\nMFR must be approved before you can build product from it. Approving a\nnew MFR also creates its Finished Product item in Item Master, so you\nnever create finished-product items by hand.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Finished Product: the batch is built.</strong> A finished\nproduct batch is built from an approved MFR and draws on QC-approved raw\nmaterial stock. The app takes the oldest approved stock first and\nreserves it at once. When production is finished, you complete the\nbatch, and it goes through its own QC review before it can be packed,\nlabelled or certified.</p>\n<p><strong>6. Packaging: the batch is issued.</strong> Finished product\nand packaging materials are issued to Store, R&amp;D or Production, so\nyou can see what left the warehouse and why.</p>\n<p><strong>7. Certificate of Analysis and Label Printing: the paperwork\nis produced.</strong> Once a batch (raw material or finished product)\nhas cleared QC, you can generate its Certificate of Analysis from the\ntests and specifications set for that item. You can also print its\nshop-floor labels (Approved Raw Material, Under Test, In-process,\nFinished Product) from the same batch and QC data. Neither screen\nchanges any data; they only format what’s already recorded.</p>\n<p>Two things run alongside the chain rather than as steps in it.\n<strong>Reports and the Dashboard</strong> show live totals of\neverything above, so you don’t have to visit each module. The\n<strong>Audit Log</strong> keeps a permanent history that nobody can\nedit: who created, changed or deleted records (QC decisions, purchase\norders, production, packaging, masters, user roles) and who signed\nin.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"what-your-role-lets-you-do\" class=\"level2\">\n<h2>What your role lets you do</h2>\n<p>Everyone signed in to Invento can <strong>view</strong> nearly\neverything; you don’t need special access to look something up. Your\nrole(s) decide what you can <strong>add, edit, delete or\napprove</strong>. You can hold more than one role, and a System Admin\nmanages roles on the <a href=\"#user-roles--access\">User Roles &amp;\nAccess</a> screen.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inventory Manager</strong>: creates and submits purchase\norders, manages Item Master/Vendor Master, records wastage in the\nInventory Ledger, and contributes to Finished Product and\nPackaging.</li>\n<li><strong>System Admin</strong>: the broadest role. Can do everything\nthe other roles can, plus actions nobody else has: managing user\naccounts, roles and display names; deleting master-data records;\nreopening a submitted purchase order (before QC has started on it);\nclosing or reopening Opening Stock and undoing a load; changing Company\nDetails and the automatic sign-out time; running the Stock Integrity\nCheck; and running Purge Test Data. System Admins are never signed out\nautomatically. If something is blocked and you don’t know why, ask a\nSystem Admin.</li>\n<li><strong>Super Auditor</strong>: a read-focused role with one\nspecific extra: access to the Audit Log (alongside System Admin).</li>\n<li><strong>Quality Checker</strong>: performs Round 1 of the two-round\nQC review, starts QC on new batches, records wastage, and can act on\nCertificates of Analysis, SOP/STP Documents, Equipment, and Dead\nStock.</li>\n<li><strong>QC Reviewer</strong>: performs Round 2 (the final decision,\nincluding setting the expiry date) of the two-round QC review, plus the\nsame start-QC, wastage and COA/Documents/Equipment/Dead Stock access as\nQuality Checker. The same person can never do both QC rounds on the same\nbatch.</li>\n<li><strong>MFR Manager</strong>: creates and approves MFRs (recipes),\nbuilds Finished Product batches, and contributes to Item Master,\nPackaging, and Equipment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A handful of things are open to <strong>any</strong> signed-in user\nregardless of role, because they’re either informational or\nself-service: viewing every module, submitting Tester Feedback, printing\nlabels, generating reports, loading Opening Stock while loading is open,\nand managing your own Profile.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"overview\" class=\"level2\">\n<h2>Overview</h2>\n<section id=\"dashboard\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Dashboard</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Overview → Dashboard</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> Every signed-in user. It’s the home\npage, and viewing it needs no particular role.</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> The Dashboard is the first screen you\nsee. It sums up the whole business at a glance: stock, purchase orders\nand QC checks in progress, and how production is going. It reads live\nfrom the same data as every other screen, so it’s always current.</p>\n<p><strong>How to check what needs your attention today</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Open Invento. You land on the Dashboard.</li>\n<li>The first card, <strong>My tasks</strong>, shows how many records\nwait for you, from your roles (for example QC records waiting for Round\n1, or batches to complete). Click a line to open its screen.</li>\n<li>If anything needs action, warning cards follow: <strong>Low\nstock</strong> (raw materials whose usable quantity has dropped below\ntheir reorder threshold), <strong>Finished product batches in\nprogress</strong> (batches started but not yet completed),\n<strong>Retest overdue or due in the next 90 days</strong>, and\n<strong>Expired or expiring in the next 90 days</strong>. A card only\nappears when there’s something to flag.</li>\n<li>Below them is the row of summary cards (Raw materials, Vendors, MFR\ndefinitions, Finished batches, POs in the last 30 days, Pending QC).\nClick any card to jump straight to that module.</li>\n<li>Click any item in the warning cards to go straight to the relevant\nscreen (Item Master, Finished Product or Quality Control).</li>\n<li>Scroll down to see four charts: inventory movement in and out over\nthe last 30 days (one chart for each unit), QC results by status,\npurchase value over the last 30 days, and finished batches produced over\nthe last 30 days.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>How it connects to the rest of the app</strong> You don’t\nenter anything here. Every number and chart reads live from records in\nItem Master, Vendors, Purchase, QC, MFR and Finished Product. There’s no\nseparate reporting step: the Dashboard shows a change on your next\nvisit.</p>\n<p><strong>Good to know</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The “Hide legacy data” checkbox in the top-right corner is a\npersonal display setting, not a delete. It hides old records (LEG- codes\nand batches loaded through Opening Stock) from lists, alerts and\ndropdowns across the app, in your browser only, and you can switch it\nback any time.</li>\n<li>Warning cards appear only when there’s something to flag. No cards\nbetween My tasks and the summary cards is a good sign, not a fault.</li>\n<li>The “Pending QC” count includes records waiting at either QC round,\nso it can be higher than the number not yet checked at all.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section id=\"master-data\" class=\"level2\">\n<h2>Master data</h2>\n<p>Master data is the base everything else builds on: your lists of\nitems, vendors, equipment and item categories. Set them up, or check\nthey exist, before you record a purchase, run QC or build a recipe.</p>\n<section id=\"item-type-master\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Item Type Master</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Master data → Item Type Master</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> Everyone can view it. Only System\nAdmin, Inventory Manager, or MFR Manager can add or edit item types.\nDeleting an item type is System Admin only.</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> Item types are the categories you\ngive items in Item Master (for example “Herb Powder” or “Bottle Cap”).\nThis screen is a flat list of those categories: add them here first,\nthen pick from them when you create an item.</p>\n<p><strong>How to add a new item type</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Go to Item Type Master. If you have write access, you’ll see an “Add\nnew item type” box directly above the list.</li>\n<li>Type a description (this is the only field) and save.</li>\n<li>The new item type appears in the list immediately, marked Active,\nand is now available to pick from when creating or editing items.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>How to edit or deactivate an item type</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Click the item type’s name in the list to open it.</li>\n<li>Change the description, or untick the Active checkbox to retire it\nwithout deleting it (an inactive item type stays in the list with an\nInactive badge, but won’t show up as a choice on new items).</li>\n<li>Save.</li>\n<li>System Admins also see a Delete button here, which asks for\nconfirmation before permanently removing the item type.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Fields you’ll be asked for</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description</strong>: the category name. It must be unique\n(capitals, extra spaces and a final full stop are ignored), so adding\none that already exists gives an error.</li>\n<li><strong>Active</strong>: only appears on the edit screen; unticking\nit hides the item type from pickers elsewhere without deleting its\nhistory.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How it connects to the rest of the app</strong> Every item in\nItem Master can be tagged with one item type, and several bulk-upload\ntemplates (Item Master, MFR) let you reference an item type by its exact\ndescription. The list shows both active and inactive item types\ntogether, since the whole point of this screen is to manage that\ntoggle.</p>\n<p><strong>Good to know</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>You can’t delete an item type that any item uses. Move those items\nto another type first, or deactivate the item type instead.</li>\n<li>Adding a description that already exists (even with different\ncapitalization is fine, but an exact duplicate isn’t) will be rejected\nwith a clear message rather than creating a duplicate.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"item-master\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Item Master</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Master data → Item Master</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> Everyone can view it. Only System\nAdmin, Inventory Manager, or MFR Manager can add or edit items. Deleting\nan item is System Admin only.</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> The master list of every raw material\nand packaging item Atharva stocks: names, codes, units, barcodes and\ncurrent stock. Purchase, the Inventory Ledger, QC and Manufacturing all\nbuild on it. Finished Product items aren’t created here; the MFR screen\ncreates them.</p>\n<p><strong>COA template column:</strong> the list shows, for every raw\nmaterial and finished product, whether its Certificate of Analysis\ntemplate is <strong>Defined</strong> or <strong>Missing</strong>. A\nMissing row has an <strong>Add</strong> link that opens the item’s page\n(raw material) or the MFR’s page (finished product) at the template\ncard. A finished product with no MFR shows “No MFR”; packaging shows a\ndash (no COA). A certificate cannot be issued for a batch until its\ntemplate exists. You can sort by the column, and the Excel download\nincludes it.</p>\n<p><strong>How to add a new item</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Go to Item Master and click “New item.”</li>\n<li>Choose a Category: Raw material or Packaging. (You can’t create a\nFinished Product item here; approving an MFR creates it.)</li>\n<li>Fill in the Name and any other fields that apply, then save.</li>\n<li>You’ll land on the item’s detail page with its item code already\nassigned, and a one-time “successfully added” banner confirms it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>How to edit an item</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>From the list, click the item’s code or name to open its detail\npage.</li>\n<li>Change any field except the item code, which is permanent, and the\ncategory once it is fixed. A Finished Product item’s category is always\nlocked. A Raw material or Packaging item’s category locks once the item\nhas purchases, recipes, QC records or stock movements; if you picked the\nwrong one, create a new item.</li>\n<li>Save. Changes take effect immediately; there’s no approval\nstep.</li>\n<li>System Admins also see a Delete button here, which asks for\nconfirmation before permanently removing the item.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Fields you’ll be asked for</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Item code</strong>: shown as a read-only preview while\ncreating (e.g. “RM-006” for a raw material; after RM-999 it carries on\nas RM-1000); the real code is only assigned once you save, and it never\nchanges afterward.</li>\n<li><strong>Botanical Name</strong>: an optional alternate/scientific\nname for the raw material.</li>\n<li><strong>Category</strong>: Raw material or Packaging only when\ncreating; it shows locked and read-only for Finished Product items and\nfor any item that already has records.</li>\n<li><strong>Active</strong>: un-ticking it is refused while the item\nstill has stock on hand (write the stock off or use it up first); the\nform also tells you when the item is used in recipes.</li>\n<li><strong>Item type</strong>: dropdown pulled from Item Type Master’s\nactive entries; required for a Raw material.</li>\n<li><strong>Barcode</strong>: optional, 3 to 40 plain characters (no\naccents or emoji), and must be unique across all items; if filled in,\nthe item’s detail page shows it as a scannable barcode image.</li>\n<li><strong>Low stock threshold</strong>: optional. When stock falls\nbelow it, the item gets a “Low stock” badge. For a raw material the\ncheck uses <strong>usable</strong> stock (approved, in date, not due for\nretest), so material awaiting QC or expired doesn’t hide a\nshortage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How it connects to the rest of the app</strong> Purchase, QC,\nthe Inventory Ledger, MFR and Packaging all use the items on this list:\nevery purchase line, QC sample and MFR ingredient belongs to one of\nthem. Stock on hand is calculated from the purchases, usage and wastage\nrecorded elsewhere; you never enter it here.</p>\n<p><strong>Good to know</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The newest items come first, so one you’ve added appears at the\ntop.</li>\n<li>QC, Stability and R&amp;D sample quantities are no longer set here.\nYou enter them on the Purchase screen when a batch arrives.</li>\n<li>You can’t delete an item that has any purchase, QC, inventory,\nproduction or MFR history. Untick Active instead.</li>\n<li>Both active and inactive items show in the list, so you can always\nfind and reactivate something you’d previously turned off.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"vendor-master\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Vendor Master</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Master data → Vendor Master</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> Everyone can view it. Only System\nAdmin or Inventory Manager can add or edit vendors. Deleting a vendor is\nSystem Admin only.</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> The master list of the suppliers you\nbuy raw materials and packaging from, with their contact details and\nvendor code. You pick from it when you create a purchase order.</p>\n<p><strong>How to add a new vendor</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Go to Vendor Master. If you have write access, an “Add new vendor”\npanel sits right next to the list, showing you the vendor code it’s\nabout to assign (e.g. “V-0093”).</li>\n<li>Fill in the Name (required) and any contact details you have:\naddress, mobile, phone and email.</li>\n<li>Save. You stay on the page, the new vendor appears in the list and a\nmessage confirms it, so you can add several vendors in a row.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>How to edit a vendor</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Click the vendor’s name or code in the list to open its detail\npage.</li>\n<li>Change any field and save. A message on the page confirms it.</li>\n<li>To stop using a vendor (for example one that failed an audit or\nstopped trading), untick <strong>Active</strong> and save. The vendor is\nno longer offered on new purchase orders, but every earlier order and\nits history stay. On the list, tick <strong>Show inactive</strong> to\nsee these vendors; tick <strong>Active</strong> again to bring one\nback.</li>\n<li>System Admins also see a Delete button here, which asks for\nconfirmation before permanently removing the vendor.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Fields you’ll be asked for</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vendor code</strong>: auto-assigned; shown as a preview\nwhile adding, and as the real, permanent code once saved.</li>\n<li><strong>Email</strong>: optional, but must look like a valid email\naddress if you enter one.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How it connects to the rest of the app</strong> Every\npurchase order is tied to a vendor from this list, so a vendor has to\nexist here before you can record a purchase from them.</p>\n<p><strong>Good to know</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vendors can be made inactive (untick <strong>Active</strong>), and\nan inactive vendor cannot be chosen on a new purchase order. Inactive\nvendors are hidden on the list until you tick <strong>Show\ninactive</strong>. Vendor names must be unique (capitals and extra\nspaces are ignored).</li>\n<li>You can’t delete a vendor that has a purchase order on file.\nReassign or remove those orders first.</li>\n<li>The vendor code on the Add form is a preview, not a reservation. If\ntwo people add a vendor at the same time, the code you get on save is\nalways correct, even if the preview was briefly out of date.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"instrument-equipment-master\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Instrument / Equipment Master</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Master data → Instrument /\nEquipment Master</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> Everyone can view it. System Admin,\nInventory Manager, MFR Manager, Quality Checker and QC Reviewer can add\nor edit equipment; equipment and calibration matter to both production\nand QC. Only a System Admin can delete a record.</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> The register of instruments and\nequipment in Atharva’s rooms: what each one is, where it is, its asset\ntag and its calibration status. It’s a reference list; it doesn’t affect\nstock or production figures.</p>\n<p><strong>How to add equipment</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Go to Instrument / Equipment Master. If you have write access, an\n“Add” form sits above the list.</li>\n<li>Fill in the Name at minimum; add Room No., Section, Asset ID,\nQuantity, and calibration details if known.</li>\n<li>Save. The new row appears in the list, sorted by Room and then by\nits equipment code.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>How to edit equipment</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Click a row in the list to open its detail page.</li>\n<li>Change any field, including calibration status and dates, and\nsave.</li>\n<li>To retire an instrument, untick <strong>Active</strong> and save; it\nis hidden on the list until you tick <strong>Show\ninactive</strong>.</li>\n<li>System Admins also see a Delete button here, which asks for\nconfirmation before permanently removing the record.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Fields you’ll be asked for</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Equipment code</strong>: auto-assigned (e.g. “EQ-0001”),\nshown as a preview while adding.</li>\n<li><strong>Asset ID</strong>: the tag printed or engraved on the\ninstrument, if it has one. This field must be unique, not the Name:\nseveral rows can be called “Wooden Barrels”, each with its own Asset\nID.</li>\n<li><strong>Calibration status</strong>: Calibrated / Due / Not\nApplicable; leave blank if it doesn’t apply yet.</li>\n<li><strong>Last calibration date / Next calibration due</strong>: when\nthe instrument was last checked and when it’s due again. The last\ncalibration can’t be in the future, and the next due date must come\nafter it. A due date in the past is allowed and means overdue.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How it connects to the rest of the app</strong> This is a\nreference list for equipment records. The Dashboard shows a card for\nequipment whose calibration is overdue or due within 30 days.</p>\n<p><strong>Good to know</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Names may repeat. Each row’s Asset ID, if it has one, must be\ndifferent.</li>\n<li>Calibration status follows the dates: Needs calibration rows need a\nNext due date, and the list shows Calibrated, Due in N days or\nOverdue.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"dead-stock-register\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Dead Stock Register</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Master data → Dead Stock\nRegister</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> Everyone can view it. System Admin,\nInventory Manager, MFR Manager, Quality Checker, and QC Reviewer can all\nadd or edit records. Deleting a record is System Admin only.</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> The register of assets that are\nwritten off or no longer in use: what they cost, how much they have\ndepreciated and what value is left. It’s reviewed and updated by hand a\nfew times a year.</p>\n<p><strong>How to add a dead stock record</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Go to Dead Stock Register. If you have write access, an “Add” form\nsits above the list, showing you the asset code it’s about to\nassign.</li>\n<li>Fill in the Name of the article and whatever purchase/depreciation\ndetails you have.</li>\n<li>Save. The row appears in the list, sorted by asset code.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>How to update a record (e.g. after a write-off)</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Click the row in the list to open its detail page.</li>\n<li>Update Balance qty and Balance value by hand when something changes;\nthe app doesn’t calculate them.</li>\n<li>Fill in Resolution date, Rejected qty, and Rejected value if the\nentry is being written off, and save.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Fields you’ll be asked for</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Purchase price</strong>: this is the price per unit, not the\ntotal for the whole quantity.</li>\n<li><strong>Depreciation %</strong>: defaults to 25% if left blank when\nbulk-uploading; on this screen it’s a plain number you enter.</li>\n<li><strong>Depreciated value / unit</strong>: calculated from Purchase\nprice and Depreciation %: purchase price × (100 − depreciation %). You\ndon’t enter it.</li>\n<li><strong>Balance qty / Balance value</strong>: entered and updated by\nhand; the app does not keep a running ledger of these for you.</li>\n<li><strong>Rejected qty / Rejected value</strong>: record how much was\nwritten off and its value, when that happens; this is also a manual\nentry, not a formula.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How it connects to the rest of the app</strong> This register\nis separate from the Inventory Ledger: it doesn’t read or change stock\non hand anywhere else in the app.</p>\n<p><strong>Good to know</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Balance qty and Balance value don’t change when you record a\nrejection. Update them yourself.</li>\n<li>Write-offs here have no review or approval step; it’s an editable\nregister.</li>\n<li>Because the figures are typed by hand, the form shows an amber\nwarning when Rejected qty, Balance qty and the values don’t add up. It\nnever blocks the save. The list’s Check column, and the Excel file,\nrepeat it.</li>\n<li>The purchase date and resolution date cannot be in the future, and\nthe resolution date cannot come before the purchase date. To retire a\nrecord untick <strong>Active</strong>; it is hidden until you tick\n<strong>Show inactive</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section id=\"procurement\" class=\"level2\">\n<h2>Procurement</h2>\n<section id=\"purchase\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Purchase</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Procurement → Purchase</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> Everyone signed in can view purchase\norders. Only a System Admin or Inventory Manager can create a purchase\norder, add/edit/delete its lines, or submit it. Once an order has been\nsubmitted, only a System Admin can reopen it, and only until QC starts\non one of its batches (see “How to submit” below).</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> Record goods received from a vendor:\none purchase order per vendor invoice, one line per item. A purchase\norder starts as a Draft you can edit. Its stock joins inventory only\nwhen someone clicks <strong>Submit order</strong>; nothing on a Draft\nchanges stock.</p>\n<p><strong>How to create a purchase order and add items</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Go to Procurement → Purchase and start a new purchase order. Enter\nthe vendor (only active vendors are listed), invoice number, invoice\ndate and <strong>Received on</strong> date. Received on is the day the\ngoods arrived; it starts as today and prints as Date of Receipt on the\nraw material label. Neither date can be in the future. The order number\nis assigned automatically.</li>\n<li>On the same screen, use the “Add line” form to add each item\nreceived: pick the item, and its batch number is generated for you\nautomatically. Enter the quantity received and confirm the unit (it\ndefaults from the item).</li>\n<li>Enter the QC, Stability and R&amp;D sample quantities and the sample\nunit for the line. Samples are set aside for testing and don’t count as\nusable stock. All four fields are required: if an item needs no sample,\nenter <strong>0</strong>, not blank. The form fills in 0 when you pick\nthe item, so you rarely type it.</li>\n<li>Enter the unit price and GST % and save the line. Both are required:\nenter <strong>0</strong> if there is none (GST % can be 0 to 100). Each\nline amount is rounded to the paisa, and the order total adds up the\nrounded lines. You don’t enter an expiry date here; the QC Reviewer sets\nit when the batch is approved. Repeat for each item on the invoice, or\nuse Bulk Data Upload for many lines. While the order is a Draft, you can\nedit or delete any line.\n<ul>\n<li>If the quantity is 10 or more times the last purchase of the same\nitem, a red note appears under the form and <strong>Add line</strong>\nstays off until you tick “Yes, this quantity is correct”. It only checks\nfor an extra zero; a large order that’s right saves as normal.</li>\n<li>The app also refuses values that can’t be right: a quantity above\n1,000,000 kg or ltr (1,000,000,000 g or ml, 100,000,000 nos or other\ncounts), a unit price above ₹10,000,000, a name over 200 characters or a\nnote over 2,000 characters. The message names the field to correct.</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Correcting invoice details:</strong> on a Draft order (or one\nthat was reopened), click <strong>Edit invoice details</strong> to fix\nthe invoice number, invoice date or received-on date. The vendor can be\nchanged only while the order has no lines. A submitted order stays\nlocked; ask a System Admin to reopen it first.</p>\n<p><strong>How to submit a purchase order so stock becomes\navailable</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>When every line is correct, click <strong>Submit order (locks\nlines)</strong> on the order’s page, then <strong>Yes, submit</strong>\nin the red box that asks once more. You submit the whole order, not one\nline at a time.</li>\n<li>Submitting adds each line’s remaining quantity (received quantity\nless its QC, Stability and R&amp;D samples) to inventory and locks the\norder, so its lines can’t be edited or deleted.</li>\n<li>If something was submitted by mistake, a System Admin can click\n<strong>Reopen</strong>, which reverses the stock that was pushed and\nunlocks the order for editing. Resubmitting later re-pushes whatever the\n(possibly corrected) lines say at that point.</li>\n<li>Reopen is <strong>refused</strong> once QC has a record on any batch\nof the order (even one still waiting for its first review), once a batch\nhas been used in production or written off, or when QC has rejected a\nbatch of the order. The message names the batch. After QC has started,\nthe order can’t be changed.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Fields you’ll be asked for</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Invoice date / Received on</strong>: both required and\nneither can be in the future. Received on is the day the goods arrived\nand is printed as Date of Receipt on the Approved Raw Material label.\nBoth can be corrected on a Draft order with <strong>Edit invoice\ndetails</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Unit price / GST %</strong>: both required on every line (0\nis allowed); GST % is 0 to 100.</li>\n<li><strong>QC Qty / Stability Qty / R&amp;D Qty</strong>: how much of\nthe received quantity is being set aside for each kind of testing.\nRequired on every raw-material line; enter 0 if none is needed for that\nline.</li>\n<li><strong>Batch number</strong>: generated automatically the moment\nyou pick the item; you never type this yourself.</li>\n<li><strong>Remaining quantity</strong>: shown once the line is saved;\nthe quantity received less the three sample quantities.</li>\n<li><strong>Raw Material / Packaging Item</strong>: choose which kind of\nline you’re adding. Packaging lines skip the QC/Stability/R&amp;D sample\nfields entirely, since packaging is never sampled.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How it connects to the rest of the app</strong> A Draft order\ndoesn’t show anywhere else in the app: Quality Control’s new-batch\npicker, the wastage form and the RM stock report list batches from\n<strong>submitted</strong> orders only. Submitting an order creates the\nbatch, which then goes through Quality Control before it can be used in\nmanufacturing.</p>\n<p><strong>Good to know</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The QC, Stability and R&amp;D quantities and unit are required. You\ncan’t leave them blank, but 0 is fine for a line that needs no\nsample.</li>\n<li>Reopening a submitted order reverses real stock, so only a System\nAdmin can do it, after a two-step confirmation, and only before QC has\nstarted on the order’s batches.</li>\n<li>After a line is saved, you can’t change its item, batch number or\nunit; only the quantity, price and sample amounts. Once the line’s batch\nhas a QC record, the quantity and samples are fixed too, and only the\nprice and GST % can change. If you picked the wrong item, delete the\nline and add it again (while the order is a Draft).</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"inventory-ledger\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Inventory Ledger</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Procurement → Inventory Ledger</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> Everyone signed in can view all\neight tabs (Ledger, Stock Position, Stock Statement, RM Report, Rejected\nMaterials, Batch Trace, Expiry &amp; Retest Ageing, Retained Samples).\nRecording wastage is limited to System Admin, Inventory Manager, Quality\nChecker, or QC Reviewer.</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> The record of every stock movement in\nInvento: material in from Purchase, QC samples, material used for\nfinished product, packaging issued and wastage. It also shows where\nstock stands: Stock Position (on hand now), RM Report As On Date (raw\nmaterial stock today) and Stock Statement (opening and closing stock for\na period). Everything here comes from other modules except wastage,\nwhich you record on this screen.</p>\n<p><strong>How to check current stock on hand</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Go to Procurement → Inventory Ledger and open the <strong>Stock\nPosition</strong> tab.</li>\n<li>Each item shows its current on-hand quantity, with a red “Low stock”\nbadge if it’s fallen below that item’s threshold, and a short breakdown\nline (e.g. Received / QC / Stability / R&amp;D / FP use / Wastage)\nshowing where the quantity came from or went. For raw materials,\n<strong>Usable</strong> shows how much production can actually use\ntoday, and <strong>Not usable</strong> shows the rest of what is on hand\nand why (awaiting QC, retest due, expired).</li>\n<li>Click an item’s name to open its page: the full breakdown, its\nbatches and a ledger of that item’s movements.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>How to record wastage</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>From any tab in Inventory Ledger, click <strong>Record\nwastage</strong>.</li>\n<li>Choose the item, then the batch it comes from; the list shows each\nbatch’s remaining quantity. A batch is required.</li>\n<li>Enter the quantity, check the unit and give a reason (required).\nSubmit. The quantity comes off that batch’s stock at once.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Fields you’ll be asked for</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Batch</strong>: required. It ties the wastage to a specific\nreceived lot.</li>\n<li><strong>Reason</strong>: a short required note on why the material\nwas wasted (spillage, damage, expiry, etc.).</li>\n<li><strong>Running balance</strong> (Ledger tab column): the item’s\nstock after that row’s movement, so you can follow the balance line by\nline.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How it connects to the rest of the app</strong> This module\nfollows everything else. Submitting a purchase order creates the Stock\nIn rows here, starting a QC check creates the sample Stock Out rows, and\nFinished Product and Packaging add their own rows when batches are made\nor issued. Wastage is the only entry you create here. The RM Report As\nOn Date, the Ledger and Stock Position show batches from\n<strong>submitted</strong> purchase orders only; a Draft order’s stock\ndoesn’t appear.</p>\n<p><strong>RM Report</strong> Open the <strong>RM Report</strong> tab\nfor a batch-by-batch list of raw material. <strong>PQTY</strong> is the\nquantity purchased, <strong>SQTY</strong> the samples taken (QC,\nstability and R&amp;D) and <strong>QTY</strong> the stock left today.\nRejected batches are not listed (see Rejected Materials). For stock on a\npast date use the Stock Statement tab.</p>\n<p><strong>Rejected Materials</strong> Open the <strong>Rejected\nMaterials</strong> tab to see every batch QC has rejected, raw material\nand finished product, with the QC, Stability and R&amp;D samples taken\nfrom each on the same row. You can search it and export it to Excel or\nPDF. When QC rejects a raw material batch, what’s left of it is taken\nout of stock on hand (the ledger shows “QC Rejected”), so Stock Position\ncounts only usable material. Wastage recorded against a rejected batch\ncomes off the rejected quantity. A purchase order with a rejected batch\ncan’t be reopened.</p>\n<p><strong>Stock Statement</strong> Open the <strong>Stock\nStatement</strong> tab to see how stock moved over a period. Choose a\n<strong>From</strong> and <strong>To</strong> date (and a category if\nyou like) and press <strong>Apply</strong>. Each item shows its\n<strong>Opening</strong> stock at the start of the From day; what came\nin (Purchased, Produced, Other in); what went out (Used in production,\nPackaging, Samples, Wastage, Rejected, Other out); and its\n<strong>Closing</strong> stock at the end of the To day. For one day,\nuse the same date twice. With To set to today, Closing matches On hand\non Stock Position. <strong>Excel</strong> and <strong>PDF</strong>\nexport the rows on screen with the period printed. <strong>View\nledger</strong> opens the Ledger for that item and period.</p>\n<p><strong>Expiry &amp; Retest Ageing</strong> Open the <strong>Expiry\n&amp; Retest Ageing</strong> tab to see every approved batch that is\nexpired, overdue for retest or coming up: raw material with stock left,\nraw material made in production, and finished product. The boxes at the\ntop group them (Expired, Retest overdue, Within 30 days, 31 to 60, 61 to\n90, Over 90 days); click one to show only that group. Each row shows the\nretest date, the expiry date and which comes first.\n<strong>Excel</strong> and <strong>PDF</strong> export what’s on\nscreen.</p>\n<p><strong>Retained Samples</strong> Open the <strong>Retained\nSamples</strong> tab to see the QC, Stability and R&amp;D samples set\naside from each batch, with the batch’s QC status and expiry date. For\nraw material, <strong>Stability left</strong> is the stability sample\nnot yet used by a retest. Search, <strong>Excel</strong> and\n<strong>PDF</strong> work as on the other tabs.</p>\n<p><strong>Batch Trace</strong> Open the <strong>Batch Trace</strong>\ntab to follow one batch for a recall or an inspection. Type a batch\nnumber (or part of it) and press <strong>Find batch</strong>, then click\n<strong>Trace</strong> on the batch you mean. You can search raw and\npacking material batches, raw material made through production, and\nfinished product batches (full or short number).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Made from</strong> lists what the batch was made from, back\nto the raw material, with the vendor and invoice.</li>\n<li><strong>Went to</strong> lists where it went: the finished product\nbatches that used it, their QC records and COA, each packaging issue\n(Store, R&amp;D or Production) and the packing material used, and any\nsamples held, wastage or quantity QC rejected.</li>\n<li>The strip at the top shows how many finished product batches were\nreached, the packs issued per department, and what is still in stock.\nClick <strong>Trace this</strong> on any batch to follow that one\ninstead. <strong>Excel</strong> and <strong>PDF</strong> export the\nwhole trace.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Stock In / Stock Out</strong> The ledger’s Event column shows\n<strong>Stock In</strong> when stock is added (receipts, batch yields),\n<strong>Stock Out</strong> when stock is taken (samples, production use,\nissues), and <strong>Wastage</strong> when it is written off.</p>\n<p><strong>Good to know</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The Ledger tab shows the 1,000 most recent events. To find something\nolder, use the item, reason and date filters.</li>\n<li>The <strong>RM Report</strong> shows each batch’s quantity as it is\n<strong>today</strong>. It has no date box, because a date would suggest\nit can show past stock. To see how stock moved over a past period, use\nthe Stock Statement tab, which gives Opening and Closing stock for any\ndates you choose. Use the Export PDF button to save or print the\nreport.</li>\n<li>Wastage always needs a batch. If an item has no received batch yet,\nthe form won’t let you submit.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section id=\"quality-control-documents\" class=\"level2\">\n<h2>Quality Control &amp; Documents</h2>\n<section id=\"quality-control\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Quality Control</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Quality Control &amp; Documents →\nQuality Control</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> Everyone signed in can view Assign\nRecords (ARs). A System Admin, Inventory Manager, Quality Checker or QC\nReviewer can start a new AR (assign a batch for QC). Only a Quality\nChecker can do the first review round and only a QC Reviewer the second,\nfinal round; a System Admin can do either. Nobody but a System Admin can\ndo both rounds on the same batch.</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> Quality Control is Invento’s most\nimportant checkpoint: raw material that hasn’t cleared QC can’t be used\nto make anything. Every batch received through Purchase gets a QC check\nand must pass <strong>two rounds of review</strong> by two different\npeople before its stock can go into production. A batch that’s rejected,\nawaiting review or overdue for retest is blocked; the app enforces this,\nnot just the screen.</p>\n<p><strong>Finished product batches in progress.</strong> When a\nfinished product batch is started (Create batch), it shows in the amber\n<strong>Finished Product Batches In Progress</strong> card at the top of\nthe Quality Control page and on the Dashboard, so QC can plan in-process\ntesting. Click a row to open the batch. When the batch is completed, it\nmoves to Finished Product Awaiting QC.</p>\n<p><strong>How to start QC on a newly received batch</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Go to Quality Control &amp; Documents → Quality Control. Batches\nthat arrived and aren’t assigned for QC yet show in the green\n<strong>Awaiting QC</strong> card at the top.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Start QC</strong> on a row, or use <strong>New\nAR</strong> and pick the item and batch yourself. The sample quantity\nand unit come from the purchase line; check them and adjust if needed.\nYou don’t enter the expiry date here; the QC Reviewer sets it in Round\n2.</li>\n<li>Submit. The batch gets an Assign Record (AR) with an\n<strong>Analytical Report No.</strong> (for example ARRM-0001/26 for raw\nmaterial, ARFP-0001/26 for finished product), and its status becomes\nAwaiting QC, ready for Round 1.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>How to review and approve or reject a batch</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Round 1 (Quality Checker):</strong> Open the AR. With the\nbatch still “Submitted,” you’ll see the Round 1 form.\n<ul>\n<li>For a raw material bought from a vendor and for a finished product,\nthe form lists the tests on the item’s (or the MFR’s) <strong>COA\ntemplate</strong>. Type the <strong>result</strong> of every test. If\nthe item has no COA template yet, the page says so and links to where it\nis added; until then you cannot approve (you can still reject).</li>\n<li>Choose <strong>Approved</strong> or <strong>Rejected</strong> and\ngive a <strong>reason</strong> (at least 3 characters). A reason is\nneeded for either decision and is kept for audit.</li>\n<li>If <strong>Approved</strong>, the status becomes <strong>Round 1\napproved — awaiting review</strong> and the AR moves to the QC\nReviewer’s queue. Round 1’s decision and results are then\nread-only.</li>\n<li>If <strong>Rejected</strong>, you are only\n<strong>proposing</strong> the rejection (you are asked to confirm\nfirst). The status becomes <strong>“Round 1 rejection proposed”</strong>\nand the batch stays in stock until a QC Reviewer decides.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><strong>Round 2 (QC Reviewer):</strong> Open the same AR.\n<ul>\n<li>On an <strong>approved</strong> Round 1, you see the Quality\nChecker’s results next to boxes for the final results. Leave them\nunchanged to keep Round 1’s results. <strong>If you change any, your\nresults are final</strong> and go on the Certificate of Analysis;\nchanged results are highlighted and both versions are kept for audit.\nChoose <strong>Approved</strong> or <strong>Rejected</strong> and give a\nreason (at least 3 characters; say why if you changed results). When\napproving, also enter the <strong>Expiry date</strong> and a\n<strong>Retest Period (days)</strong>. For raw material the retest\nperiod starts at 180 days (6 months), the longest allowed, and you can\nshorten it. A rejection asks you to confirm.</li>\n<li>On a <strong>proposed rejection</strong>, either <strong>Confirm the\nrejection</strong> (the batch is rejected and leaves stock) or\n<strong>Send it back</strong> to the Quality Checker with a reason.\nSending back returns the AR to Awaiting QC and Round 1 starts again; the\nearlier decision stays in the history.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>Once Round 2 is decided, the record is final: both decisions and\nboth sets of results are read-only, with the <strong>Decision\nhistory</strong> below. Only a batch Approved in <strong>both</strong>\nrounds can be used to make finished product.</li>\n<li><strong>Voiding a rejection:</strong> a QC Reviewer or System Admin\ncan open a rejected AR and use <strong>Void this rejection</strong> with\na written reason. The AR returns to Awaiting QC, the stock comes back on\nhand, and a finished product batch returns to “Submitted to QC”. It is\nnot possible once wastage has been written off against the batch.</li>\n<li>The <strong>Certificate of Analysis</strong> takes its results from\nQC (the Reviewer’s if he edited any, otherwise the Quality Checker’s)\nand shows them locked. A record that was decided before results were\nrecorded keeps typing them on the certificate.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Fields you’ll be asked for</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sample Quantity / Unit</strong>: filled in from the purchase\nline; check it and confirm.</li>\n<li><strong>Expiry date</strong>: Round 2 only, and required when\napproving. It cannot be in the past. On a retest it cannot be later than\nthe expiry the batch was last approved with.</li>\n<li><strong>Retest Period (days)</strong>: Round 2 only, when approving.\nYou enter it because how long a material stays good varies with the\nmaterial and the test results. For raw material it starts at 180 days,\nwhich you can shorten but not exceed. The app works out the retest date\nfrom it.</li>\n<li><strong>Comments</strong>: a short note explaining the decision, at\nboth rounds.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How it connects to the rest of the app</strong> Only a fully\nApproved batch (both rounds, in date and not overdue for retest) can be\npicked for a Finished Product batch; the app blocks every other status.\nWhen a batch’s retest date arrives, it appears in the <strong>Due for\nretest</strong> card on this page. <strong>Start retest</strong> takes\nthe sample you enter from the stability sample reserved at Purchase\n(then from stock), and the batch goes through both review rounds\nagain.</p>\n<p><strong>Good to know</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>More roles can assign QC (New AR) than can decide it. Only the\nQuality Checker and QC Reviewer can do the review rounds; a System Admin\ncan stand in for either.</li>\n<li>The same person can’t approve both rounds on the same batch. If you\ndid Round 1, you won’t be able to do Round 2 on that same AR.</li>\n<li>A batch at Round 1 approved — awaiting review isn’t usable yet; it\nneeds Round 2. Treat a batch as clear only when it shows Approved.</li>\n<li>A retest AR is marked with a small “Retest” badge so you can tell it\napart from a batch’s first-ever QC check.</li>\n<li><strong>Two people, one record.</strong> If two people decide the\nsame record at the same moment, the first decision is saved. The second\nperson sees “Someone else has just made this decision” and nothing of\ntheirs is saved. Refresh the page to see the result.</li>\n<li><strong>Rejected raw material</strong> leaves On hand automatically\n(see Rejected Materials in the Inventory Ledger).</li>\n<li><strong>Retest limits for raw material.</strong> After its first\napproval, a raw material batch can be retested at most <strong>three\ntimes</strong>, each retest period at most 180 days, so a batch is\ncovered for up to about 18 months. On the <strong>third retest</strong>\nthe QC Reviewer sets only the Expiry date: there’s no retest period, and\nthe material can be used until that date without another retest. A batch\npast its expiry date is never retested; it shows as Expired and Start\nretest is refused. The retest date can’t fall after the expiry date, so\na period that runs past it is refused; enter a shorter one.</li>\n<li><strong>Finished product follows the same retest cycle.</strong> An\napproved finished product batch is retested when its retest date\narrives: at most three times, at most every 180 days, as for raw\nmaterial. The QC page shows a <strong>Finished product due for\nretest</strong> card. Enter the sample and press <strong>Start\nretest</strong>. The sample comes from the batch’s stability sample\nfirst, then from its bulk; bulk taken comes off the batch and off On\nhand. The retest goes through both rounds and gets its own ARFP number\nand certificate. While a retest is open, after the retest date, or after\nexpiry, the batch <strong>can’t be packed or labelled</strong>. If a\nretest is rejected, the bulk still in stock leaves On hand; packs\nalready made stay as they are. A QC Reviewer or System Admin can void\nthe rejection, which brings the bulk back.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"label-printing\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Label Printing</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Quality Control &amp; Documents →\nLabel Printing</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> Any signed-in user. Printing a label\ndoesn’t save or change anything, so the screen needs no particular\nrole.</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> Prints the standard shop-floor labels\n(Approved Raw Material, Under Test, In-process, Finished Product) from\ndata already recorded in Purchase, Quality Control and Finished Product.\nThe output matches the paper templates used on the floor, ready to print\nand cut.</p>\n<p><strong>How to print labels</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Go to Label Printing.</li>\n<li>Pick the <strong>Label type</strong>: Approved Raw Material, Under\nTest, In-process, or Finished Product.</li>\n<li>Type part of the item name or the batch number in the\n<strong>Search</strong> box, then pick the batch from the list under it.\nThe list shows the newest matching batches that can have this label, at\nmost 50; if it says more exist, type more of the name or number to\nnarrow it.</li>\n<li>Check the preview. It shows the label layout with your batch’s\ndetails filled in.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Download PDF</strong>. You get a print-ready sheet of\ncopies of the same label: 6 per page (2×3) for Approved Raw Material,\nFinished Product and In-process, or 10 per page for Under Test.</li>\n<li>Print, cut the labels apart, and apply them.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Fields you’ll be asked for</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Label type</strong>: determines the layout, wording, and\nwhich fields appear.</li>\n<li><strong>Record</strong>: the purchase batch (raw material labels) or\nfinished product batch (Finished Product label) the label’s details are\npulled from.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A few fields have no source data in the app yet, so they print as a\nblank line to fill in by hand: <strong>Retest Period</strong> (when the\nQC record has none), <strong>In-process Start Date</strong>, and the\n<strong>Sign</strong> line on every label.</p>\n<p><strong>How it connects to the rest of the app</strong> This screen\nonly reads data entered elsewhere: batch, vendor and invoice details\nfrom Purchase, approval status and retest period from Quality Control,\nand batch and expiry details from Finished Product. It changes none of\nit.</p>\n<p><strong>Good to know</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Each label lists only the batches it fits. Approved Raw Material and\nFinished Product: batches QC-approved right now (a finished product\nbatch that is expired, past its retest date or being retested is left\nout). Under Test: batches awaiting a QC decision or due for retest.\nIn-process: any batch that isn’t rejected. The search runs on the\nserver, so the page stays fast however many batches there are.</li>\n<li>Only a PDF download is available; JPEG export has been removed for\nall four label types.</li>\n<li>The Raw Material label lists show raw material purchases only, not\npackaging purchases.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"certificate-of-analysis\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Certificate of Analysis</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Quality Control &amp; Documents →\nCertificate of Analysis</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> Anyone signed in can view\ncertificates. Creating a certificate, and setting up test templates, is\nlimited to System Admin, Quality Checker, and QC Reviewer.</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> Generates an official Certificate of\nAnalysis (COA) for a Raw Material or Finished Product batch that has\nalready passed Quality Control. You enter the actual test results\nagainst a fixed list of tests for that item type, and the app produces a\nprint-ready, letterhead-formatted PDF certificate.</p>\n<p><strong>How to generate a certificate</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Go to Certificate of Analysis and click <strong>New\nCOA</strong>.</li>\n<li>Choose whether you’re certifying a <strong>Raw Material</strong> or\na <strong>Finished Product</strong> batch.</li>\n<li>Search for and select the specific batch. Only batches that have\nalready been <strong>Approved</strong> in Quality Control show up in\nthis list.</li>\n<li>If the item (or, for finished product, the MFR) has no COA template\nyet, you see a message with a link to add one instead of the form. A\ntemplate must exist before you can generate a certificate.</li>\n<li>Review the certificate header (batch number, dates, quantities and\nso on). The fields are filled in from the batch and QC record, and you\ncan edit any of them; some wording, such as the sampled quantity, is\nmeant to be adjusted by hand.</li>\n<li>For each row in the Test Results table, enter the actual\n<strong>Result</strong>. The S.No., Test, and Specification columns come\nfrom the item’s (or MFR’s) template and can’t be changed here.</li>\n<li>Check the <strong>Remarks</strong> line at the bottom. It starts as\n“The above sample complies/Not complies as per IHS.” Edit it to fit this\nbatch, for example by deleting whichever of “complies” or “Not complies”\ndoesn’t apply.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Generate certificate</strong>.</li>\n<li>On the certificate’s own page, click <strong>Download PDF</strong>\nto get the final letterhead certificate. Long product names and wording\nwrap inside their box, and if the Remarks and signature lines do not fit\nunder the test table they move together to a new page. (Analyzed-by /\nApproved-by lines are left blank for a physical signature.)</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Setting up test templates</strong> Every raw material and\nevery finished-product MFR has its own COA template: a list of Tests and\nSpecifications. You can define it when you add the item or MFR, or later\non the item or MFR page (card “Certificate of Analysis template”).\nSerial numbers are assigned automatically. Saving replaces the list, and\neach save is kept in the template’s history. Whoever can edit the item\n(or MFR) can edit its template. The <strong>COA Template\nRegister</strong> (link on the Certificate of Analysis list) lists every\nitem and MFR with its template status, number of tests and last change,\nand exports to Excel and PDF.</p>\n<p>To set up templates for many items at once, use <strong>Admin → Bulk\nData Upload</strong> and pick Certificate of Analysis Templates. Fill in\nCode (raw material item code or MFR code), Test and Specification in one\nspreadsheet.</p>\n<p><strong>Fields you’ll be asked for</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Raw Material / Finished Product</strong>: which kind of\nbatch you’re certifying; this determines which picker and which template\n(recipe-based vs. raw item) is used.</li>\n<li><strong>Batch picker</strong>: only lists batches already marked\nApproved by Quality Control; a batch that’s still pending or under test\nwon’t appear.</li>\n<li><strong>Certificate header fields</strong>: filled in from the batch\nand QC record (batch number, product, AR number, quantities, dates) and\nlocked; only the Sampled Qty wording can be edited. The system checks\nthem again when you save and refuses a certificate whose header or tests\ndiffer from the batch or the template.</li>\n<li><strong>Result</strong>: the actual value/finding you record for\neach test on the template; Test and Specification themselves are\nread-only, copied from the template.</li>\n<li><strong>Remarks</strong>: the closing statement printed at the\nbottom of the certificate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How it connects to the rest of the app</strong> A batch can\nbe certified only once it is Approved in Quality Control. Templates\nbelong to the individual raw material or MFR. A few older certificates,\ncreated before this screen existed, show a link to an external file\ninstead of a preview and PDF.</p>\n<p><strong>Good to know</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>If a batch you expect is missing from the list, it probably isn’t\nApproved in QC yet. Check Quality Control first.</li>\n<li>Editing a template never changes a certificate already generated:\neach certificate keeps a permanent copy of the tests and results it was\nmade with.</li>\n<li>Templates set up on the item or MFR page and through Bulk Data\nUpload are stored in the same place.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"sop-stp-documents\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>SOP / STP Documents</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Admin → SOP / STP Documents</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> Anyone signed in can view and open\ndocuments. Uploading a new document is limited to System Admin, Quality\nChecker, and QC Reviewer.</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> A searchable library of Standard\nOperating Procedure (SOP) and Standard Testing Procedure (STP)\ndocuments. It lists links to files with revision numbers; it has no\nreview or approval step. Store the document elsewhere (for example on a\nshared drive) and record its link, type, title and revision here.</p>\n<p><strong>How to add a document</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Go to SOP / STP Documents and click <strong>New\ndocument</strong>.</li>\n<li>Choose the <strong>Document type</strong>: SOP or STP.</li>\n<li>Enter the <strong>Title</strong>.</li>\n<li>Enter the <strong>Revision number</strong> (defaults to 0).</li>\n<li>Paste the <strong>File URL</strong>: the link to where the file is\nstored. You can’t upload files here, only links.</li>\n<li>Optionally set the <strong>Effective date</strong>.</li>\n<li>Save. You’ll be returned to the list with a confirmation\nbanner.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Fields you’ll be asked for</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Document type</strong>: SOP or STP; shown as a badge in the\nlist and searchable by typing “sop” or “stp” in the list’s search\nbox.</li>\n<li><strong>File URL</strong>: a link to the actual document file, not\nan upload; clicking the file link on the list opens it in a new\ntab.</li>\n<li><strong>Revision number</strong>: a plain integer you increase\nyourself when a document is updated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How it connects to the rest of the app</strong> This module\nstands alone: it doesn’t read or change data in Purchase, QC or\nManufacturing. It keeps your SOP and STP references in one place, with\ntheir revisions.</p>\n<p><strong>Good to know</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>There’s no edit screen. To update a document, add a new entry with a\nhigher revision number; the old revision stays in the list as a\nrecord.</li>\n<li>Make sure the File URL opens for everyone who uses Invento.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"audit-log\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Audit Log</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Reports &amp; checks → Audit\nLog</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> View-only, and restricted to System\nAdmin and Super Auditor. Everyone else who opens the page sees an\n“Access restricted” message instead of the log.</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> A read-only history of who changed\nwhat, and when, across the app: every create, edit and delete on\nmasters, purchasing, QC, production, packaging, finished products, COA,\nstock corrections, company settings, opening stock, user roles and\naccounts. It shows the values before and after each change, not only who\nlast changed a record.</p>\n<p><strong>How to look up a change</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Go to Audit Log.</li>\n<li>Narrow the list with the filters at the top: <strong>Table</strong>,\n<strong>Action</strong> (created, edited, deleted), <strong>Changed\nby</strong>, <strong>Record</strong> (type a batch, AR or PO number, or\nan item or vendor code) and a <strong>From / To</strong> date range. The\nlist updates as you choose.</li>\n<li>Each row shows when, the table, the record, the action, who made the\nchange and how, and a short summary of the fields that changed (for\nexample “quantity 10 → 12”). Click a column heading to sort the\npage.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>View</strong> on a row to open its detail page: a full\nbefore-and-after comparison, with field names in words and people,\nitems, vendors, batches and recipes shown by name.</li>\n<li>The list shows 200 changes at a time, newest first; use\n<strong>Older</strong> and <strong>Newer</strong> at the bottom to move\nthrough them.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>QC decisions:</strong> choose Table → <strong>QC Decision\nHistory</strong> to see each round’s decision, reason and who made it,\none row per step (Round 1, Round 2, proposed rejection, send back,\nvoid).</p>\n<p><strong>Sign-ins:</strong> choose Table → <strong>User\nAccount</strong> to see who signed in and out, failed sign-in attempts\n(the e-mail typed, never the password) and passwords changed by the\nuser. A run of failed attempts on one account is worth a look. After 20\nfailed attempts for one e-mail in an hour (or 200 in all), the log adds\none line saying more were not recorded.</p>\n<p><strong>History on a record’s own page:</strong> the Item, Purchase\nOrder, QC record, MFR and Finished Product batch pages show a\n<strong>History</strong> button (System Admin and Super Auditor only)\nthat opens the Audit Log for that one record.</p>\n<p><strong>Download:</strong> the <strong>Excel</strong> button above\nthe list saves the page on screen. <strong>Download all matching\n(Excel)</strong> at the bottom saves every change that matches your\nfilters, up to the newest 10,000; the file says if there were more. Both\nfiles have Before and After columns.</p>\n<p><strong>Fields you’ll be asked for</strong> This screen is read-only:\nthe filters are the only thing to fill in.</p>\n<p><strong>How it connects to the rest of the app</strong> The log\ncovers every business record in the app, and every entry links back to\nthe real record it is about. Nobody can edit or delete a log row.</p>\n<p><strong>Good to know</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The Record box matches what you type against the record’s own\nreference (batch, AR or PO number, code or name) or its full internal\nid. It doesn’t search other fields.</li>\n<li>If the page says “Access restricted”, your role isn’t System Admin\nor Super Auditor. Ask an admin if you need audit access.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section id=\"manufacturing\" class=\"level2\">\n<h2>Manufacturing</h2>\n<section id=\"mfr\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>MFR</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Manufacturing → MFR</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> Everyone signed in can view MFRs.\nOnly System Admin or MFR Manager can create, edit, or approve one.</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> A Manufacturing Formula Record (MFR)\nis the approved recipe for a finished product: which raw materials, and\nhow much of each, go into one batch. Approving an MFR also creates its\nFinished Product item in Item Master, so every finished product starts\nhere.</p>\n<p><strong>How to create and approve an MFR</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Go to Manufacturing → MFR and start a new one. Give it a name and,\nif it’s for an already-existing finished product, link it; otherwise a\nnew Finished Product item is created for you on approval.</li>\n<li>Add one ingredient line per raw material: pick the item and enter\nthe quantity per batch.</li>\n<li>Save as Draft while you’re still working on it. You can edit a Draft\nfreely.</li>\n<li>When it’s ready, click <strong>Approve</strong>. This locks the\nrecipe (no further editing of ingredient lines) and, if this is a new\nfinished product, creates its Item Master entry automatically with an\nFP- item code.</li>\n<li>To change an approved recipe, create a new version instead of\nediting it. That keeps a permanent record of which recipe version each\nproduction run used.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Fields you’ll be asked for</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ingredient item + quantity per batch</strong>: one line per\nraw material. Quantities scale automatically when you build a batch of a\ndifferent size from the recipe’s base batch size.</li>\n<li><strong>Version</strong>: MFRs are versioned; each approved version\nis kept, not overwritten, so historical batches always point to the\nexact recipe they were actually made with.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How it connects to the rest of the app</strong> An MFR must\nbe Approved before you can build a Finished Product batch from it, and\napproving it creates its Finished Product item in Item Master; you never\ncreate that item by hand. Every Finished Product batch records the MFR\nversion it was built from.</p>\n<p><strong>Good to know</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>An MFR loaded through Bulk Data Upload arrives as a Draft. Someone\nmust open it and click Approve before it can be used, as for one entered\nby hand.</li>\n<li>An approved recipe’s ingredient lines can’t be edited. Make a new\nversion instead, so earlier production runs stay linked to the recipe\nthey used.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"finished-product\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Finished Product</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Manufacturing → Finished\nProduct</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> Everyone signed in can view finished\nproduct batches. Only System Admin, MFR Manager, or Inventory Manager\ncan compose a new batch.</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> Build a finished product batch: the\napp draws QC-approved raw material stock according to an approved MFR,\nand the new batch then goes through its own Quality Control before it\ncan be used.</p>\n<p><strong>How to build a finished product batch</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Go to Manufacturing → Finished Product and start a new batch.</li>\n<li>Pick the approved MFR (recipe) you’re building against, the batch\nsize and the batch start date.</li>\n<li>The next screen works out the quantity of each ingredient and takes\nthe raw material <strong>automatically, oldest QC-approved stock first\n(FIFO)</strong>, drawing from more than one batch when needed. You do\nnot pick batches yourself. Only batches that are fully Approved in QC\nand not overdue for retest or expired are used. If an ingredient has not\nenough approved stock, the screen says so and the batch cannot be\ncreated.</li>\n<li>Submit. The batch is created as a <strong>Draft</strong>, and the\nraw material is reserved from inventory straight away.</li>\n<li>A Draft is not yet in production. Click <strong>Create\nbatch</strong> to start production (the batch becomes In Progress and\nshows on the Dashboard and the Quality Control page), or\n<strong>Cancel</strong> to release the raw material. A Draft left for\nmore than <strong>30 minutes</strong> cancels itself and the reserved\nraw material goes back to inventory; a countdown on the batch shows the\ntime left.</li>\n<li>When production is finished, open the batch and use <strong>Complete\nbatch</strong>. Enter the batch yield, finish date, expiry date, sample\nunit and the QC, Stability and R&amp;D sample quantities. The form shows\nthe yield as a percentage of the batch size, with the range the MFR\nallows. If the yield is outside that range, write a reason, which QC can\nsee. A yield above 150% of the batch size is refused as a likely typing\nmistake. A review step lets you check everything before it’s saved. The\nbatch then shows <strong>Complete - Awaiting QC</strong>, and an\n<strong>After completion</strong> card appears on its page.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Submit to QC</strong>. The batch gets an Analytical\nReport No. (like ARFP-0001/26) and goes through its own Quality Control,\nlike a raw material batch. It can be packed, labelled or certified only\nafter QC approves it. The expiry date QC approves prints as Best Before\non the label.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Fields you’ll be asked for</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>MFR / recipe</strong>: must be an Approved MFR; Draft\nrecipes aren’t selectable.</li>\n<li><strong>Batch size and batch start date</strong>: the batch size\nscales every ingredient quantity from the recipe automatically.</li>\n<li><strong>Complete Batch</strong>: batch yield, finish date, expiry\ndate, sample unit, and the QC, Stability and R&amp;D sample quantities\n(all required). The finish date cannot be in the future or before the\nstart date, and the batch start date cannot be in the future.</li>\n<li><strong>Batch number</strong>: a full number such as FP-00001-01/26,\nplus a short one beside it: PR-01/26 for a Domestic product or OR-01/26\nfor an Export product (set by the Market chosen on the MFR). Screens\nshow both; printed documents and Reports show the short one.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How it connects to the rest of the app</strong> Finished\nProduct needs an approved recipe (MFR) and approved raw material batches\n(Quality Control). It feeds Packaging, which issues the finished batch,\nand Certificate of Analysis and Label Printing, once the batch has its\nown QC approval.</p>\n<p><strong>Good to know</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A raw material batch that’s pending, rejected, expired or overdue\nfor retest is never used. If a batch you expected isn’t being used,\ncheck its QC status first.</li>\n<li><strong>Retest of a finished batch.</strong> An approved batch is\nretested when its retest date arrives (see Quality Control). The batch\npage lists every QC record: <strong>First QC</strong>, then\n<strong>Retest 1</strong>, <strong>Retest 2</strong> and <strong>Retest\n3</strong>. While a retest is open, the batch cannot be packed or\nlabelled, and the bulk left shown on the page already has the retest\nsample taken off.</li>\n<li>The raw material is set aside as soon as the Draft is saved, not\nafter the finished batch’s own QC passes. If the Draft is cancelled, by\nyou or after 30 minutes, the stock goes back.</li>\n<li><strong>After completion card.</strong> On a completed batch’s page,\nit shows the samples taken, how much bulk packaging has used, how much\nis left unpacked, and each packing run with a link to Packaging.</li>\n<li><strong>No correction after Complete.</strong> Once a batch has been\ncompleted, its yield, dates and sample quantities cannot be changed on\nthe screen, so use the review step to check them first.</li>\n<li>QC sees a batch as soon as it starts: it shows in the\n<strong>Finished Product Batches In Progress</strong> card on the QC\npage and the Dashboard until it is completed.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"packaging\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Packaging</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Manufacturing → Packaging</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> Everyone signed in can view\npackaging issue records. Only System Admin, Inventory Manager, or MFR\nManager can record a new issue.</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> Record finished product packed and\nissued to Store, R&amp;D or Production, and the packaging material used,\nso there’s a record of what left, how much and why. Every issue is a\npack; there’s no repack or unpack.</p>\n<p><strong>How to record a packaging issue</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Go to Manufacturing → Packaging and start a new issue.</li>\n<li>Choose the <strong>department</strong> (Store, R&amp;D or\nProduction) and the <strong>Issue date</strong>. The Issue date is\nrequired and starts as today; you can enter an earlier date, but not a\nfuture one.</li>\n<li>For each line (Store or R&amp;D), pick the <strong>approved finished\nproduct batch</strong> being packed, the <strong>pack size</strong>\n(quantity and unit, for example 200 ml) and the <strong>number of\npacks</strong>. The number of packs cannot use more than what is left of\nthat batch.</li>\n<li>Add the <strong>packaging materials</strong> used (material,\nquantity and unit). They are taken from their purchase batches oldest\nfirst.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Save</strong>, then <strong>Yes, save</strong> in the\nred box that asks once more. The issue and its materials are saved\ntogether, each line gets its own <strong>PKG-</strong> code, and the\nfinished product and packaging material come off stock at once.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Packing several pack sizes at once (Store/R&amp;D):</strong>\nafter choosing Store or R&amp;D, add one line for each pack size or\nbatch (for example 200 ml × 50 and 1 ltr × 8 from the same batch), up to\n30 lines in one save. Each line has its own packaging materials and gets\nits own packaging issue code. The Issue date and department are shared.\nThe form shows the bulk product each line uses and what is left in each\nbatch; if any line can’t be saved, none are.</p>\n<p><strong>Issuing several batches to Production at once:</strong>\nchoose Production and add one line per Finished Product batch, up to 30\nlines in one save. Each line has the quantity to convert, the QC,\nStability and (optionally) R&amp;D quantities, and the sample unit. Each\nline becomes its own Raw Material (RM-FP) batch with its own QC and\npackaging issue code. A batch can be on one line only: if you repeat it,\na red message appears under that line and Add line and Save stay greyed\nout until you fix it. Nothing is saved unless every line is valid.</p>\n<p><strong>Fields you’ll be asked for</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Department</strong>: Store, R&amp;D or Production.</li>\n<li><strong>Finished product batch</strong>: only approved batches are\noffered.</li>\n<li><strong>Pack size and number of packs</strong>: capped at what is\nleft of the batch.</li>\n<li><strong>Packaging materials</strong>: material, quantity and unit\nfor each line.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How it connects to the rest of the app</strong> Packaging\nuses the same stock the Inventory Ledger tracks: an issue here shows\nthere as an outgoing movement. For a finished batch it’s the last step\nbefore it leaves Atharva, apart from the certificate and labels that go\nwith it.</p>\n<p><strong>Voiding an issue made by mistake</strong> (Inventory Manager\nor System Admin)</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>On the Packaging list, click <strong>Void</strong> in the issue’s\nStatus column.</li>\n<li>A red box lists what goes back: the bulk to its batch, each\npackaging material to the batches it came from, and the packs taken off\nPackaged Finished Product stock.</li>\n<li>Give a reason (at least 3 characters) and click <strong>Yes,\nvoid</strong>. Nothing is deleted: the stock goes back through new\nledger rows, and the issue stays on the list struck through, with a\n<strong>Void</strong> badge, the date, who voided it and why.</li>\n<li>A Production issue can be voided only while its RM-FP batch hasn’t\nbeen used in a finished product batch or written off. Its RM-FP batch\nthen leaves stock and its QC record is marked Void.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Good to know</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>You can’t issue more than a batch has available; the form stops you\nbefore stock goes negative. When a batch or packaging material is short,\na red “Not enough stock for …” message names the line and shows what’s\navailable against what you asked for. The quantity box turns red, and\nAdd line and Save stay greyed out until it’s fixed.</li>\n<li>There is no repack or unpack: every issue is a pack.</li>\n<li><strong>Retest blocks packing.</strong> A finished product batch\ncannot be packed while it is expired, past its retest date, or while a\nretest of it is open on the Quality Control page. The form says why.\nWhen the retest is approved the batch can be packed again; a rejected\nretest takes the remaining bulk out of stock (packs already made stay).\nSamples taken for a retest come off the bulk, so they reduce what is\nleft to pack.</li>\n<li>The Packaging list has a Packing Register PDF for printing.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section id=\"admin\" class=\"level2\">\n<h2>Admin</h2>\n<section id=\"user-roles-access\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>User Roles &amp; Access</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Admin → User Roles &amp;\nAccess</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> Only System Admins see the\nmanagement tools: creating accounts, resetting passwords, changing roles\nand disabling accounts. Anyone else who opens the page sees “Access\nrestricted”.</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> A System Admin controls here who can\nsign in and what each person may do: creating accounts (nobody can\nregister themselves), assigning one or more of the six roles (Inventory\nManager, System Admin, Super Auditor, Quality Checker, QC Reviewer, MFR\nManager), resetting a forgotten password and disabling the account of\nsomeone who leaves. A person’s roles decide which Add and Edit buttons\nand forms they see in every module.</p>\n<p><strong>How to add a new person</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>As a System Admin, go to User Roles &amp; Access and use the\nadd-user form: enter the person’s name, their sign-in email and a\ntemporary password (at least 10 characters).</li>\n<li>Save. The screen shows the email and temporary password once; give\nthem to the person privately. They are asked to choose their own\npassword the first time they sign in.</li>\n<li>Tick the roles the person needs and save (see below).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>How to change someone’s roles</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>You’ll see a list of every user, one row each, with a set of six\ncheckboxes showing their current roles.</li>\n<li>Tick or untick roles for the person you want to change.</li>\n<li>Click Save on that person’s row. The change applies at once. Saving\nreplaces all of that person’s roles with the boxes ticked now, so if you\ntick only one box, their other roles are removed.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Automatic sign-out.</strong> The card above the user list\nsets how long a person can be inactive before Invento signs them out:\ntick the box and enter 5 to 480 minutes (60 by default), or untick it to\nswitch the sign-out off. It applies to every role except System\nAdmin.</p>\n<p><strong>Change name:</strong> each person’s row has a <strong>Change\nname</strong> button (System Admin only). Names are at most 100\ncharacters and no two people can share one (capitals and extra spaces\nare ignored).</p>\n<p><strong>Other actions on a person’s row:</strong> <strong>Reset\npassword</strong> sets a new temporary password they must change at\ntheir next sign-in. <strong>Disable account</strong> signs them out and\nstops them signing in; <strong>Enable</strong> switches the account back\non. Both ask you to confirm.</p>\n<p><strong>Fields you’ll be asked for</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Role checkboxes</strong>: Inventory Manager, System Admin,\nSuper Auditor, Quality Checker, QC Reviewer, MFR Manager. A person can\nhold any combination, including none.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How it connects to the rest of the app</strong> Every “Add,”\n“Edit,” and “Delete” control throughout Invento checks the roles\nassigned here before showing itself. Changing someone’s roles on this\nscreen immediately changes what they can do everywhere else, without\nthem needing to log out and back in.</p>\n<p><strong>Good to know</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>People are listed by name, not email. Make sure you recognise the\ndisplay name before changing someone’s access.</li>\n<li>You cannot remove your own System Admin role, and you cannot disable\nyour own account; the last remaining System Admin can never be removed\nor disabled. Ask another System Admin if you need that done.</li>\n<li>Saving a row replaces all of that person’s roles; you can’t add one\nrole without the others being ticked too. Check every box before\nsaving.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"reports\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Reports</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Reports &amp; checks → Reports</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> Every signed-in user can view and\nexport reports. The screen is read-only, so it needs no particular\nrole.</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> The four registers Atharva needs for\nday-to-day tracking and audits: raw material stock, QC history, finished\nproduct history and purchases. Each is a searchable table you can filter\nby date and download as Excel or PDF.</p>\n<p><strong>How to pull a report and export it as a PDF</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Go to Reports &amp; checks → Reports.</li>\n<li>Scroll to the report card you need: <strong>RM Stock\nReport</strong>, <strong>QC Register</strong>, <strong>FP\nRegister</strong>, or <strong>Purchase Register</strong>.</li>\n<li>Optionally, narrow it down using the <strong>From</strong> /\n<strong>To</strong> date fields on that card (see below for which date\neach one filters on).</li>\n<li>Use the table’s built-in search to find specific rows on screen, and\nclick a column heading (where it shows the up/down mark) to sort.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Download Excel</strong> or <strong>Download\nPDF</strong> to export the rows that pass your date filter. The search\nbox only narrows what you see on screen; it does not change what is\ndownloaded.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Fields you’ll be asked for</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>From / To (date range)</strong>: appears on every report\ncard. Each report filters by a different underlying date, since each has\na different natural “event date”:\n<ul>\n<li>RM Stock Report filters by the date the item was added.</li>\n<li>QC Register filters by the date the QC result was reviewed.</li>\n<li>FP Register filters by the finished batch’s finish date.</li>\n<li>Purchase Register filters by the date the purchase was\nreceived.</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How it connects to the rest of the app</strong> Every report\nreads live from other modules: RM Stock Report from Item Master and\ncurrent stock, QC Register from Quality Control, FP Register from\nFinished Product, and Purchase Register from Purchase and Vendor Master.\nYou don’t create or edit anything here. If a number looks wrong, correct\nit in the source module and the report follows.</p>\n<p><strong>Good to know</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The date filter narrows the rows already on screen; it doesn’t run a\nnew search, so applying and clearing it is instant.</li>\n<li>The downloads follow the date filter but not the search box, so set\nthe date range first if you want a narrower export.</li>\n<li>Download PDF gives a printable table with the letterhead. Lists with\nmore than 7 columns print sideways on A4 so codes and quantities stay on\none line. The rupee sign and letters with marks (for example Brahmī)\nprint correctly, and Hindi names print too, without joined letter forms.\nA PDF holds up to 3,000 rows (about 80 pages); for a longer list the app\nasks you to narrow it (a date range or the search box) or use Download\nExcel. Download Excel gives a spreadsheet with real numbers and dates,\nso you can sort and total them.</li>\n<li>The RM Stock Report shows <strong>On Hand</strong> and\n<strong>Usable Now</strong> (approved, in date, not due for retest) side\nby side. Its flag says “Below threshold” when the usable stock is under\nthe item’s low stock threshold. It shows current stock only; for stock\non a past date use the Stock Statement tab in the Inventory Ledger.</li>\n<li>The Purchase Register lists submitted purchase lines only (drafts\nare not stock yet), with the unit and the quantity still remaining. The\nQC Register has a Type column (Purchased RM, Production RM or Finished\nproduct), the expiry date, and the retest date of each decision.</li>\n<li>Batches loaded through Opening Stock show a Legacy tag on screen and\na Source column in the downloads.</li>\n<li>QC Register and FP Register are full history logs and show every\nrow, including old and inactive ones. The Vendor, Equipment and Dead\nStock lists, by contrast, hide inactive records until you tick Show\ninactive.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"tester-feedback\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Tester Feedback</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Admin → Tester Feedback</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> Any signed-in user can submit\nfeedback from any page. Only System Admins can review and respond\n(category, status and notes); anyone else who opens Admin → Tester\nFeedback sees that it needs System Admin access.</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> Report bugs, confusing screens or\nsuggestions from the page where you noticed them, without leaving the\napp. Each submission gets a ticket number (like FB-0007) so it can be\ntracked, and a System Admin can sort it and leave a note the person who\nsent it will see.</p>\n<p><strong>How to submit feedback from any page</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>On any screen, scroll to the bottom and click <strong>Feedback &amp;\nchange log</strong> to open it.</li>\n<li>Type what you noticed (a bug, something confusing or a suggestion)\nin the box, in at least a few words.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Submit</strong>. You’ll see a confirmation and your\nnew ticket appear in the list below, with a ticket number.</li>\n<li>As long as your ticket hasn’t been reviewed yet (“Awaiting review”),\nyou can click <strong>Edit</strong> to fix the wording or\n<strong>Delete</strong> to retract it. Once a System Admin has\ncategorized or responded to it, it becomes read-only for you.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Fields you’ll be asked for</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Observation</strong> (anyone): what you saw, in plain text.\nIt’s the only field a regular user fills in.</li>\n<li><strong>Category</strong> (System Admin, when sorting): Bug,\nEnhancement, Invalid request, User education, Duplicate or Other.</li>\n<li><strong>Status</strong> (System Admin, when sorting): Awaiting\nreview, Awaiting implementation, Implemented or Rejected. The sender\nsees this as the ticket’s state.</li>\n<li><strong>Notes</strong> (System Admin, when sorting): a short note\nexplaining the decision, for example why something was rejected or what\nchanged. The sender can see it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How it connects to the rest of the app</strong> The feedback\nbox is at the bottom of every page, and each submission records the page\nit came from, so a System Admin in Admin → Tester Feedback can see which\nscreen each report is about. Nothing else in the app reads this data;\nit’s a log for reporting and sorting.</p>\n<p><strong>Good to know</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A System Admin triaging feedback can filter the list by status using\nthe tabs at the top of the Tester Feedback screen (All, Awaiting review,\nAwaiting implementation, Implemented, Rejected).</li>\n<li>Ticket numbers (FB-0001, FB-0002, …) are assigned automatically in\norder and are the easiest way to refer to a specific piece of feedback\nin conversation.</li>\n<li>Purge Test Data (see below) never deletes Tester Feedback tickets,\nso your submissions survive a full test-data wipe.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"bulk-data-upload\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Bulk Data Upload</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Admin → Bulk Data Upload</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> Anyone, but you see a module’s card\nonly if you can add or edit in that module on its own screen; the same\npermissions control bulk upload. If you can’t write to any module, you\nsee a “no access” message.</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> Load many records at once from an\nExcel file instead of one at a time. It covers eight areas: Item Master,\nVendor Master, Item Type Master, MFR, Purchase, Instrument / Equipment\nMaster, Dead Stock Register and Certificate of Analysis (COA) Templates.\nEach works the same way: download a blank template, fill it in and\nupload it.</p>\n<p><strong>How to bulk-upload records</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Go to Bulk Data Upload. You’ll see one card for each area you have\naccess to.</li>\n<li>On the card for the area you want, click <strong>Download\ntemplate</strong>. The Excel file has an Instructions sheet with the\nrules, a data sheet with one example row to copy, and, for most modules,\na Reference sheet listing the valid values (such as active item codes or\nvendor names) so you don’t have to guess spellings.</li>\n<li>Fill in your rows in the data sheet, following the example row’s\nformat. Required columns are marked with an asterisk.</li>\n<li>Come back to the card, choose your filled-in file, and click\nUpload.</li>\n<li>If every row is valid, they are all imported and a message gives the\ncount. If any row has a problem, nothing is imported, and you get a list\nof every row to fix before you try again.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Fields you’ll be asked for</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Item, vendor, equipment and purchase order codes</strong>:\nleave them out of your file. The app assigns them on import, as it does\nwhen you create a record by hand.</li>\n<li><strong>MFR and Purchase files</strong>: these have multiple rows\nbelonging to one record (e.g. several ingredient lines for one MFR, or\nseveral purchase lines for one invoice). Repeat the same MFR Name (for\nMFR) or the same Vendor Name + Invoice Number (for Purchase) on every\nrow that belongs together.</li>\n<li><strong>COA Templates</strong>: one row per Test and Specification,\ngrouped by repeating the same Code (raw material item code or MFR code).\nA code that already has a template is refused; edit it on the item or\nMFR page instead.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How it connects to the rest of the app</strong> An upload\nfollows the same rules as adding a record by hand on that module’s\nscreen, and the records appear in the same places: an uploaded item in\nItem Master, an uploaded purchase order in Purchase as a Draft, and so\non.</p>\n<p><strong>Good to know</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A file can have up to 500 data rows and 800 KB. Split a larger set\ninto several files and upload them one after another.</li>\n<li>A Purchase file needs the QC, Stability and R&amp;D quantities, Unit\nPrice and GST % on every line (0 is allowed), only active vendors are\naccepted, and the invoice date cannot be in the future.</li>\n<li>An uploaded purchase order arrives as a Draft, like one entered by\nhand. Someone must open it and click Submit order before it changes\nstock.</li>\n<li>An uploaded MFR still needs someone to click Approve before it\ncreates a usable Finished Product item.</li>\n<li>If a file is refused, fix the listed rows and upload the whole file\nagain. Nothing is imported partly, and the valid rows aren’t kept.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"opening-stock\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Opening Stock</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Admin → Opening Stock</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> Anyone with a role can load opening\nstock while loading is <strong>open</strong>. Only the System\nAdministrator can close loading, re-open it, or undo a load.</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> Before go-live, the stock already on\nthe shelves is loaded from the physical stock count sheet, so the app\nstarts with the right quantities. Raw material, packaging and finished\nproduct (packed and bulk) are loaded here. The System Administrator\ncloses loading by hand after the agreed cut-off date. The app keeps no\ncut-off date of its own.</p>\n<p><strong>Two ways to enter the stock.</strong> Each card has two tabs:\n<strong>Upload Excel</strong> and <strong>Type it in</strong>. Both end\nin the same checks and the same all-or-nothing load, so use whichever\nsuits the amount of stock.</p>\n<p><strong>How to load from an Excel file</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Open the card for Raw Material, Packaging or Finished Product and\nclick “Download template”. Fill one row per batch (or lot) from the\nstock count sheet.</li>\n<li>Choose the file and click “Check file”. Every problem is listed with\nits Excel row number. Nothing is loaded while any problem remains.</li>\n<li>For Raw Material and Packaging, the check also shows a short summary\nfor each item: how many batches and the total quantity it will load.\nCompare it with your stock sheet before you load.</li>\n<li>When the check says the file is fine, click “Load N rows”. All rows\nare loaded together or not at all, and the load gets a number (OPN-0001,\nOPN-0002, …).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The <strong>Loads</strong> table at the bottom of the page lists\nevery load with its number, type, rows, QC status counts, the date and\ntime, and <strong>who loaded it</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>How to type the rows in</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Open the card and click the <strong>Type it in</strong> tab. Each\nbatch (or lot) is one row card. Pick the item from the list (typing\nnarrows it); the unit fills in from the item and can be changed.</li>\n<li>For Raw Material, choose the QC status first. Only the fields that\nstatus needs are shown: an Approved batch asks for the old AR number, QC\napproval date, retest date and retests already done; a Rejected batch\nasks for the old AR number and approval date; a Pending QC batch asks\nfor neither.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>Add row</strong> for the next batch,\n<strong>Duplicate</strong> to copy a row (the batch number and AR number\nare left empty, because those cannot repeat) and <strong>Remove</strong>\nto drop one. Empty rows are ignored.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Check rows</strong>. Problems appear inside the row\nthey belong to, with nothing loaded. When the rows are fine, click\n<strong>Load N rows</strong>. Changing anything after a check hides the\nLoad button until you check again.</li>\n<li>Up to 500 rows can be loaded at once. After a load the page clears\nso the next group can be typed.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Unit column (Raw Material and Packaging).</strong> Every row\nmust say which unit its quantity is counted in. If that is a different\nunit from the one the item is kept in but of the same kind (for example\ng for an item kept in kg), the quantity is converted for you; if it\ncannot be converted, the row is refused. An older file without the Unit\ncolumn is refused, so download the current template.</p>\n<p><strong>Raw material rows</strong> are Approved, Pending QC or\nRejected. Approved and Rejected rows carry the old Analytical Report\n(AR) number exactly as written in the old records, plus the QC approval\ndate. Approved rows also carry the retest date and the manufacturer\nexpiry date. Pending QC rows only need the expiry date and join the\nnormal QC queue. “Retests already done” (0 to 3) counts toward the limit\nof 3 retests.</p>\n<p><strong>Finished product rows</strong> carry one row per batch:\nProduct Code, Batch No, manufacture date, expiry date, the old AR number\nand the QC approval date (finished product loaded here is always\nApproved). Fill “Bulk quantity unpacked” (in the product’s own unit), or\n“Packs in stock” with “Pack size” (for example <code>100 ml</code>), or\nboth. The pack size must be in the same family as the product’s unit (ml\nwith ltr, g with kg). The batch’s total yield is the bulk plus packs ×\npack size. Packs are recorded as one Store packaging entry dated at the\napproval date, so they appear in Packaging and can be labelled or issued\nlike any other packs. The bulk stays on the batch and can still be\npacked later. Two optional columns carry the retest cycle: “Retest date”\n(after the approval date, not after the expiry date) and “Retests\nalready done” (0 to 3, counted toward the limit of 3; with 3 done leave\nthe retest date blank). A product must have an approved MFR before its\nstock can be loaded.</p>\n<p><strong>Legacy tag:</strong> A batch or AR number loaded here shows a\ngrey “Legacy” tag beside it on the QC (including Awaiting QC and Due for\nretest), Purchase, Inventory (item page and ledger), Finished Product,\nPackaging, Labels, COA, Reports and Dashboard alert screens, and a\n“Source” column in Excel exports. The QC list, RM Report, Finished\nProduct, Packaging, Purchase, Inventory ledger, COA and the three\nReports registers also have a Source filter (All / New / Legacy). In the\nbatch pickers on the Wastage, Packaging and new-batch screens the old\nbatches are marked “(Legacy)”. Batch numbers and AR numbers that look\nlike ones made by the app are refused, so an old number can never be\nmistaken for a new one.</p>\n<p><strong>Undo:</strong> While loading is open, the System\nAdministrator can undo a load if none of its stock has been used and no\nnew QC record has been started on its batches. For finished product, a\nload can no longer be undone once any of its packs have been issued, new\npackaging has been done against its batches, or a COA has been made.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"company-details\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Company Details</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Admin → Company Details</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> Everyone can see the details. Only\nthe System Administrator can change them.</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> The company name, address and licence\nnumber printed on reports, intimation slips, Certificates of Analysis,\nlabels and the Word documents (MFR and BMR). On every printout the logo\nis on top with the company name directly below it, then the address and\nlicence number.</p>\n<p><strong>To change the licence number (or the name or\naddress):</strong> open the screen, edit the box, and click Save. The\nnext report or document you download shows the new value. Files you\ndownloaded earlier are not changed. The fields are company name,\naddress, licence label (for example “Mfg. Lic. No.”) and licence number\n(for example PD/AYU/111). Each change is recorded in the Audit Log.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"purge-test-data\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Purge Test Data</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Admin → Purge Test Data</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> System Admin only. Anyone without\nthat role who opens this page sees an access-restricted message and\ncannot proceed.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Warning: this permanently deletes real data, with no undo in\nthe app.</strong> Purge Test Data deletes nearly every business record\nin Invento: every item, vendor, item type, MFR recipe, purchase order,\nquality check, finished product batch, BMR record, packaging issue,\nequipment entry, dead-stock entry and inventory ledger transaction,\nincluding all legacy (LEG-) data. You can’t reverse it from the app. Use\nit only to reset the system to blank for testing, and never without a\nbackup you can restore.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> Start testing from a completely empty\nsystem (no items, purchase orders, QC history or production records)\nwithout deleting everything by hand. It’s for setting up end-to-end test\nruns, not for routine clean-up or fixing one mistake.</p>\n<p><strong>How to purge test data</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Go to Admin → Purge Test Data (requires System Admin access).</li>\n<li>Read the warning at the top of the page. It reminds you to take a\nbackup first if you might still need anything.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>“Purge test data…”</strong> to reveal the confirmation\nform.</li>\n<li>Read the detailed warning that appears, listing everything that will\nbe deleted.</li>\n<li>In the confirmation box, type <strong>PURGE ALL DATA</strong>\nexactly. The purge button stays off until it matches.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>“Purge everything.”</strong> The button shows\n“Purging…” while it runs.</li>\n<li>When it finishes, a summary shows how many rows were removed from\neach table, and the total.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Fields you’ll be asked for</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Confirmation phrase</strong>: type\n<code>PURGE ALL DATA</code> exactly (same spacing and capitals) before\nthe delete button works. It’s a safety check against an accidental\nclick, not a password.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How it connects to the rest of the app</strong> A purge\nclears data in nearly every module at once: Item Master, Vendor Master,\nPurchase, Quality Control, Finished Product, MFR, Packaging, Equipment\nand the Dead Stock Register are empty afterwards, and the Dashboard and\nReports show zero records. Numbering for items, purchase orders, AR\nnumbers, MFR recipes, equipment and dead-stock entries also resets, so\nthe next record of each type starts again at -0001.</p>\n<p><strong>Good to know</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>This cannot be undone from inside the app</strong>: there is\nno recycle bin or restore button. Take a backup before you click it if\nthere’s anything you might need later.</li>\n<li>Accounts, every user’s roles and all Tester Feedback tickets are\nkept; the purge clears business and inventory data only.</li>\n<li>Only System Admins can even see this button in an active state;\neveryone else is blocked at the page level.</li>\n<li>Because numbering restarts at -0001, use a purge only for a full\ntesting reset, not to remove one bad entry. For that, edit or delete the\nrecord in its own module.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"stock-integrity-check\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Stock Integrity Check</h3>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Reports &amp; checks → Stock\nIntegrity Check</p>\n<p><strong>Who can use it:</strong> System Admin only. Anyone else who\nopens the page sees an access-restricted message.</p>\n<p><strong>What it’s for:</strong> Invento keeps stock in two places:\nthe inventory ledger (how much of each item there is) and the quantity\nleft on each batch. Every receipt, issue, write-off, rejection and undo\nhas to change both. This screen compares them. It runs nine read-only\nchecks: an item’s On hand against the parts on Stock Position, each\npurchase batch and each production-issued batch against the ledger,\nnegative stock, purchase lines that are not submitted but have stock,\nledger rows with the wrong unit or item, finished product stock against\nthe bulk left on approved batches, duplicate batch numbers, and orphan\nrecords. It never changes data.</p>\n<p><strong>How to use it</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Open Reports &amp; checks → Stock Integrity Check. The checks run\nevery time the page opens; reload to run them again.</li>\n<li>A green banner means every check passed. A red banner lists how many\ndifferences were found. Each failing check says what it compares and\nwhat a difference means, and shows the first 20 rows. Every row starts\nwith a sentence saying what is wrong, then the item, batch numbers, date\nand figures involved.</li>\n<li>If anything fails, do not correct stock by hand. Note the rows and\ntell the developer.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The page also shows the number of rows in the inventory ledger. The\nstock reports read the whole ledger, so they slow down as it grows; the\npage warns when it approaches 3 million rows, which is when a\nclosing-balance table should be planned.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"legacy-deprecated-modules\" class=\"level3\">\n<h3>Legacy / deprecated modules</h3>\n<p>These modules were part of Invento at one point but are no longer\nexpected to be used. They’re kept under Admin, marked “- Deprecated,” so\ntheir historical entries stay available for reference without cluttering\nthe main workflow. There’s no plan to develop them further.</p>\n<p><strong>Batch Mfg. Record- Deprecated</strong> (Archive → Batch Mfg.\nRecord- Deprecated, System Admin only): the original Batch Manufacturing\nRecord workflow (weighment lines, in-process observations, sign-off) for\na production run. The current MFR and Finished Product flow above\nreplaced it. Existing records stay visible for reference.</p>\n<p><strong>Line Clearance- Deprecated</strong> (Archive → Line\nClearance- Deprecated, System Admin only): a pre-production check that a\nproduction line was clean and ready (an Area, an optional batch\nreference and a Clear / Not clear result). Existing entries stay visible\nfor reference; new entries aren’t expected.</p>\n<p><strong>Environmental Control- Deprecated</strong> (Archive →\nEnvironmental Control- Deprecated, System Admin only): Temperature and\nHumidity readings for an Area. Readings were recorded as entered, with\nno out-of-range alert or pass/fail check. Existing readings stay visible\nfor reference.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section id=\"glossary\" class=\"level2\">\n<h2>Glossary</h2>\n<p><strong>AR (Assign Record) / Analytical Report No.</strong>: a\nQuality Control record, one per batch assigned for testing; on screen\nits number is called the Analytical Report No. Numbered\n<code>ARRM-0001/26</code> for raw material and <code>ARFP-0001/26</code>\nfor finished product (a 4-digit running number per type, then the year;\neach type starts again at 0001 every year, and after 9999 it continues\nas 10000). Numbers issued before 3 Oct 2026 keep the old form, for\nexample <code>AR-001-22092026</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Batch</strong>: a specific received lot of a raw material or\npackaging item (from one Purchase line), or a specific production run of\na finished product. Every batch has its own batch number and its own\nQC/stock history, even if it’s the same item as another batch.</p>\n<p><strong>COA (Certificate of Analysis)</strong>: the official document\ncertifying that a batch meets its specifications, generated from\nQC-approved test results. Numbered like <code>COA-0001-2026</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Draft</strong>: the editable starting state of a Purchase\nOrder or MFR before it’s submitted/approved. Nothing in a Draft affects\nstock or production until it’s finalized.</p>\n<p><strong>Expiry date</strong>: the date after which a batch must not\nbe used. It is set by the QC Reviewer at every approval (it cannot be\nbefore today) and an expired batch is blocked from use and from\nretesting.</p>\n<p><strong>FIFO (first-in, first-out)</strong>: the rule Invento applies\nwhen it takes raw material for a finished product batch: the oldest\nQC-approved stock is used first, automatically, and may be drawn from\nseveral batches.</p>\n<p><strong>Item code prefixes</strong>: every item, vendor, and other\nmaster-data record gets an automatic code so you never have to invent or\ntype one yourself:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>RM-####</code>: raw material item</li>\n<li><code>PKG-####</code>: packaging item</li>\n<li><code>FP-####</code>: finished product item (created automatically\nwhen an MFR is approved)</li>\n<li><code>V-####</code>: vendor</li>\n<li><code>MFR-####</code>: MFR (recipe) record</li>\n<li><code>PO-####</code>: purchase order</li>\n<li><code>EQ-####</code>: equipment/instrument</li>\n<li><code>DS-####</code>: dead stock register entry</li>\n<li><code>FB-####</code>: Tester Feedback ticket</li>\n<li><code>LEG-…</code>: a record imported from the legacy (pre-Invento)\nsystem, rather than created in the app</li>\n<li><code>OPN-####</code>: an Opening Stock load</li>\n<li><code>ARRM-####/YY</code> and <code>ARFP-####/YY</code>: Analytical\nReport Nos. for raw material and finished product</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Legacy</strong>: stock that was loaded through Opening Stock\nfrom the old records instead of being received and tested in Invento.\nIts batch and AR numbers are kept as they were written, and a grey\n“Legacy” tag shows beside them. The “Hide legacy data” switch and the\nSource filter (All / New / Legacy) on some lists use it.</p>\n<p><strong>MFR (Manufacturing Formula Record)</strong>: the approved\nrecipe for a finished product: which raw materials, in what quantities,\nmake one batch.</p>\n<p><strong>QC two-round review</strong>: the mandatory Quality Control\nprocess every batch goes through: Round 1 (Quality Checker) and Round 2\n(QC Reviewer), performed by two different people, both of whom must\napprove before the batch is usable.</p>\n<p><strong>Retest</strong>: a scheduled re-check of a batch after its\nRetest Period, using the stability sample set aside at Purchase. A batch\noverdue for retest is treated like one not yet approved: it can’t be\nused until it passes the retest.</p>\n<p><strong>On hand / Usable / Not usable</strong>: On hand is all the\nstock of an item in the ledger. For raw material,\n<strong>Usable</strong> is the part that production may use today (QC\napproved, not expired, not due for retest); <strong>Not usable</strong>\nis the rest, shown with the reason (awaiting QC, retest due, expired).\nLow stock alerts compare the threshold with Usable.</p>\n<p><strong>Role</strong>: one of six permission groups (Inventory\nManager, System Admin, Super Auditor, Quality Checker, QC Reviewer, MFR\nManager) assigned per person on User Roles &amp; Access; a person can\nhold several at once. See <a href=\"#what-your-role-lets-you-do\">What\nyour role lets you do</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Short batch number</strong>: the short form of a finished\nproduct batch number, such as PR-01/26 (domestic) or OR-01/26 (export),\nshown beside the full number (FP-00001-01/26) on screens and used alone\non printed documents and Reports.</p>\n<p><strong>Submit order (formerly Final Submit)</strong>: the action\nthat turns a Draft purchase order into a real, stock-affecting record.\nBefore submission, nothing on the order is visible anywhere else in the\napp.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"faq-troubleshooting\" class=\"level2\">\n<h2>FAQ &amp; troubleshooting</h2>\n<p><strong>I can’t find an “Add” or “New” button on a screen I should be\nable to write to.</strong> Your role(s) probably don’t allow changes in\nthat module. Check <a href=\"#what-your-role-lets-you-do\">What your role\nlets you do</a>, or ask a System Admin to check your roles on User Roles\n&amp; Access. Viewing is open to everyone; adding and editing depend on\nyour role in each module.</p>\n<p><strong>A batch I expect to see isn’t showing up in a picker\n(e.g. when building a Finished Product batch, generating a COA, or\nprinting a label).</strong> This is almost always a Quality Control\nstatus issue. The batch must be fully <strong>Approved</strong> (both QC\nrounds), not expired and not currently overdue for retest to appear in\nmost downstream pickers. Check its status on the Quality Control screen\nfirst.</p>\n<p><strong>I submitted a purchase order by mistake and need to fix a\nline.</strong> Only a System Admin can reopen a submitted purchase\norder; reopening reverses the stock it added and unlocks the lines. Ask\na System Admin to use <strong>Reopen</strong> on the order’s page. It’s\nrefused if QC has started on any batch of the order, a batch has been\nused or written off, or a batch was rejected; the order then stays as it\nis.</p>\n<p><strong>I made a mistake on a line in a Draft purchase\norder.</strong> You can’t change the item, batch number or unit on a\nsaved line. Delete the line and add it again, while the order is a\nDraft. Once it’s submitted, a System Admin must reopen it first.</p>\n<p><strong>I was signed out while I was away from my desk.</strong>\nInvento signs you out after a period without activity (60 minutes unless\nyour System Admin has changed it); anything not saved is lost. System\nAdmins are not signed out this way. Sign in again and re-enter the\nunsaved work.</p>\n<p><strong>The stock shows quantity on hand, but I can’t use\nit.</strong> On hand includes material that production may not use. Open\nInventory Ledger → Stock Position and look at the Usable and Not usable\ncolumns: material waiting for QC, due for retest or expired is Not\nusable. Rejected raw material is taken out of On hand and listed under\nRejected Materials.</p>\n<p><strong>It says “Someone else changed this record while you were\nediting it.”</strong> Someone saved the same item, vendor or purchase\norder details before you did, and nothing of yours was saved. Reload the\npage, look at their change, then make your edit again.</p>\n<p><strong>My bulk upload file was rejected.</strong> Nothing is\nimported if any row has a problem. The error list shows which rows and\nwhat’s wrong. Fix those rows and upload the whole file again; nothing is\nimported partly or corrected automatically.</p>\n<p><strong>I need to update an existing COA test template but bulk\nupload rejected it.</strong> Bulk upload only accepts items/MFRs that\ndon’t already have a template. To change an existing template, open the\nitem or MFR page and use Edit on its COA template card.</p>\n<p><strong>Something looks wrong on the Dashboard or a Report.</strong>\nBoth read live from the same data as every other screen; there’s no\nseparate reporting copy to fix. Correct the record in its source module\n(Item Master, Purchase, QC and so on), and the Dashboard and Reports\nfollow.</p>\n<p><strong>I noticed a bug or something confusing and want to report\nit.</strong> Scroll to the bottom of the page you’re on and use the\n<strong>Feedback &amp; change log</strong> box. It records the page you\nwere on and gives you a ticket number. See <a\nhref=\"#tester-feedback\">Tester Feedback</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Who can I ask if I’m stuck on something not covered\nhere?</strong> Ask your System Admin first about access and permissions.\nFor how a screen behaves, use the Feedback box on that screen; a System\nAdmin reads it, and it’s also how this guide gets updated.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"how-this-guide-stays-current\" class=\"level2\">\n<h2>How this guide stays current</h2>\n<p>This guide is kept up to date with the app, from the same sources the\ndevelopers use (<code>docs/modules/*.md</code> and\n<code>docs/DESIGN.md</code> in the Invento code). Its “Where to find\nit”, “Who can use it” and step-by-step instructions are checked against\nthe current screens and permissions.</p>\n<p>When Invento’s screens, menus or roles change in a way that affects\nhow you use it, this guide is updated and a new PDF is published at the\nsame link in the app (Overview → User Guide). The same page shows the\nguide’s text, opens at the right section from each screen’s\n<strong>Help</strong> link, and holds the <strong>Process Map</strong>:\ndiagrams of the flows in this guide, on screen or as a PDF. The link you\nhave always gives the latest edition. The version note at the top shows\nwhen it was last updated.</p>\n<p>If this guide is out of date, wrong or missing something, say so in\nthe <strong>Feedback &amp; change log</strong> box at the bottom of any\npage (see <a href=\"#tester-feedback\">Tester Feedback</a>). That’s the\nfastest way to get it corrected in the next update.</p>\n</section>\n";
